{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba site/src/content/methodology.md && nl -ba site/src/content/findings.md && nl -ba site/src/content/measure.md && nl -ba site/src/content/about.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Methodology\n     2\t\n     3\tTrue Base Bench measures how far a language-model checkpoint sits from a *pre-anneal pretraining base*: a checkpoint from the long first phase of pretraining, before the learning-rate anneal, mid-training data mixes and any post-training. Nothing here is a benchmark question. Every feature is a statistic of the model's own next-token distribution, read on fixed text that is the same for every model.\n     4\t\n     5\tThe measurement is reference-free. No model is scored against another model's outputs, and no answer is judged right or wrong. A pre-anneal checkpoint has been trained toward the data and nothing else, so on text it has never seen it expects as much surprise as it gets. Annealing on curated mixes, distillation, instruction tuning and preference optimisation each leave a different mark on that distribution, and the four sub-scores are built to tell those marks apart.\n     6\t\n     7\tNumbers quoted on this page are read live from the current data bundle; each one links to the bundle field it came from. Constants that define the method (ramp anchors, run lengths, thresholds) are marked as definitions.\n     8\t\n     9\t## The measurement pipeline {#pipeline}\n    10\t\n    11\tEach checkpoint goes through the same scripts (`vh_score.py` with HF transformers, or `vh_score_vllm.py` with vLLM) on the same text. There are three kinds of pass.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Teacher-forced passes.** The real document is fed in and, at every position, the exact full-vocabulary next-token distribution is reduced to five numbers: entropy H, varentropy V, surprisal s of the real next token, top-1 probability p1 and the rank of the real token. No top-k truncation is applied. Four corpora are read this way:\n    14\t\n    15\t| pass | text | size | role |\n    16\t|---|---|---|---|\n    17\t| `tf` | Pile documents (NeelNanda/pile-10k, fixed seeded selection, at least 400 characters) | 400 documents, at most 2048 tokens each, about 290k tokens | in most pretraining sets, so it also measures recall; fallback for features that need post-cutoff text |\n    18\t| `tf_wiki` | Wikipedia and GitHub documents | 150 + 60 documents | memorisation features |\n    19\t| `tf_fresh` | UncheatableEval 2026-04: AO3, arXiv, bioRxiv, BBC, GitHub, Wikipedia and others, 15 categories | 900 documents, about 1.3M tokens | written after every measured model's cutoff; drives the calibration scores |\n    20\t| `tf_weird` | rare-genre Pile documents: Enron, OpenSubtitles, HackerNews, DM Mathematics, NIH, FreeLaw, USPTO, YouTube subtitles, EuroParl, PhilPapers, Ubuntu IRC, PG-19 | 199 documents | per-genre gap and verbatim share (shown, not scored) |\n    21\t\n    22\t**Own samples.** 32 sequences from a bare `<bos>` and 32 continued from 32-token document openers, 1536 steps each (1024 for the FP8 giants, to fit memory), at temperature 1 with no truncation. Sampling continues past end-of-text. H, V, s and p1 are recorded at every step, where s is now the surprisal of the token the model itself sampled.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Surname probe.** The exact distribution over US surnames under six prompts, plus sampled lists under the same prompts (see [the surname test](#surnames)).\n    25\t\n    26\tThe analysis scripts then turn the per-position arrays into a fingerprint: `vh_analyze.py` (fingerprint features, including the calibration and prior features), `vh_memo.py` (verbatim runs), `vh_selfboot.py` (top-1 reliability and own-sample lexical statistics), `vh_tail.py` (realised tail statistics), `vh_names_table.py` (surname and rare-genre tables) and `vh_truebase.py` (sub-scores and the overall score). [Measure your model](/measure) gives the commands.\n    27\t\n    28\t## One position: entropy, varentropy, surprisal {#position}\n    29\t\n    30\tAt one position the model assigns a probability p(x) to every token x in its vocabulary. Three numbers summarise it, all in nats:\n    31\t\n    32\t- **Entropy** H = −Σₓ p(x) log p(x): the surprise the model expects.\n    33\t- **Varentropy** V = Σₓ p(x) (−log p(x) − H)²: the variance of the surprise under the model's own distribution. A distribution with one strong candidate and a long flat tail has high V; a uniform choice among a few tokens has V = 0.\n    34\t- **Surprisal** s = −log p(y) of the token y that actually came next: the surprise the model got.\n    35\t\n    36\tAlongside these the scripts store p1, the probability of the top-1 token, and the rank of y (0 when the model's top choice was right).\n    37\t\n    38\t{{explainer:position}}\n    39\t\n    40\t## Teacher forcing on a fixed corpus {#teacher-forcing}\n    41\t\n    42\tTeacher forcing feeds the real document one token at a time and records the distribution before each real token. Nothing is sampled and nothing is judged; the model's choice never changes what it sees next. Because every checkpoint reads exactly the same tokens of exactly the same documents (re-tokenised by its own tokenizer), per-corpus means are directly comparable across models with the same tokenizer and comparable in shape across tokenizers.\n    43\t\n    44\tThe own-sample pass is the opposite: the model writes, and the statistics describe the text it chose. Its surprisal is the surprisal of its own samples, so its mean equals its mean entropy in expectation; own samples are used for the prior (what the model writes from nothing) and for lexical statistics, not for calibration.\n    45\t\n    46\t{{explainer:teacher-forcing}}\n    47\t\n    48\t## The calibration gap {#calibration-gap}\n    49\t\n    50\tThe calibration gap on a corpus is\n    51\t\n    52\t> gap = mean over positions of (H − s)\n    53\t\n    54\tIf the real next token were drawn from the model's own distribution, E[s] would equal H at every position and the gap would be zero. A model trained by maximum likelihood toward a data distribution, and evaluated on fresh text from that distribution, approaches this: its expected surprise matches its realised surprise. A **negative** gap means the model is more surprised than it expected to be: it is over-confident. A positive gap means it is under-confident.\n    55\t\n    56\tRecalled text breaks this logic in the other direction (a memorised document is less surprising than the model's entropy implies), so the scored feature is read on post-cutoff text:\n    57\t\n    58\t- `F_calib_gap` = mean(H − s) over all `tf_fresh` positions. If a checkpoint has no `tf_fresh` pass, the Pile `calib_gap` is used and the model page says so.\n    59\t- `F_gap_domain_absmean` = the mean over the UncheatableEval categories of |mean(H − s) within that category|, counting each category with more than 2,000 tokens. A model can have a small overall gap by being over-confident in one genre and under-confident in another; this feature catches miscalibration that has spread across genres.\n    60\t\n    61\tIn this bundle every pre-anneal checkpoint sits within ±{{val:pre-band}} of zero on post-cutoff text, and the most negative single-domain gap of any of them is {{val:pre-domain-min}}. For comparison, specimen A ({{val:spec-a}}) reads {{val:spec-a-gap}} and specimen B ({{val:spec-b}}) reads {{val:spec-b-gap}}.\n    62\t\n    63\tAlso shown, not scored:\n    64\t\n    65\t- **ECE** of the top-1 prediction on `tf_fresh`: positions are binned by p1 into 10 equal-width bins, and ECE is the position-weighted mean of |hit rate − mean p1| across bins. The reliability diagram on each model page plots the bins.\n    66\t- **Gap where uncertain**: mean(H − s) restricted to positions with H > 4 nats.\n    67\t\n    68\t{{explainer:calibration-gap}}\n    69\t\n    70\t## Verbatim runs {#verbatim-runs}\n    71\t\n    72\tA **verbatim run** is a streak of at least {{def:RUN_MIN_LEN|count}} consecutive positions in one document where the model's top-1 prediction is the real token and its entropy is below {{def:RUN_H_MAX|num2}} nats. Inside a run the model is not predicting; it is reciting.\n    73\t\n    74\tThe memo feature `X_runfrac_wiki` is the share of tokens in the 150 Wikipedia documents of `tf_wiki` that fall inside verbatim runs. Wikipedia is in almost every pretraining set, so some recall is normal; what moves the feature is an anneal that up-weights Wikipedia-like sources and trains on them at a low learning rate. When `X_runfrac_wiki` is missing, `verbatim_run_frac_wiki`, the same share computed on the smaller set of Wikipedia documents inside the Pile selection, is used and flagged.\n    75\t\n    76\tThe same rule gives the verbatim share per genre on `tf_weird` (shown, not scored).\n    77\t\n    78\t{{explainer:verbatim-runs}}\n    79\t\n    80\t## The instruction-shaped prior {#prior}\n    81\t\n    82\t`roll_instr_marker_rate` is the share of the 32 bare-`<bos>` samples that contain at least one chat or instruction marker as a substring: role headers and chat-template tokens (`Assistant:`, `<|im_start|>`, `<|start_header_id|>`), instruction headings (`### Instruction`, `### Response`), assistant boilerplate (`As an AI`, `I hope this helps`, `Sure, here`) and reasoning-format strings (`step by step`, `Final Answer`, `\\boxed{`). The full list is `MARKERS` in `vh_analyze.py`. A pre-anneal checkpoint, asked to write from nothing, writes the web. A model whose training mix contained instruction data writes that data back, even when no prompt asks for it. With 32 samples the rate moves in steps of 1/32.\n    83\t\n    84\t## The surname test {#surnames}\n    85\t\n    86\tThe surname test (after Gwern) compares the model's distribution over US surnames with the population distribution in the 2010 US Census. It is the one probe here that has an external reference, and it separates two behaviours that the other features cannot: knowing what a \"list of common surnames\" looks like, and being able to draw from the distribution.\n    87\t\n    88\t**Exact distribution.** For the 2,000 most common Census surnames, the scripts compute log P(name | prompt) at the list slot, summing the Title-case and ALL-CAPS spellings (name tokens only, no terminator). KL and entropy are computed on the distribution renormalised over the set; the raw mass on the set is reported separately. Six prompts are used:\n    89\t\n    90\t- three **described** frames, which say in words that the list is random or frequency-weighted, and read the distribution at item 1;\n    91\t- three **few-shot** frames, which give six genuine Census draws as items 1 to 6 and read the distribution at item 7.\n    92\t\n    93\tPer frame family (the mean over its three frames) the bundle reports KL(Census ‖ model), the model's entropy over the set against the Census entropy over the same set ({{num:reference.census_entropy_top2000|nats}}), the mass on the ten most common names and the total mass on the set.\n    94\t\n    95\t**Sampled lists.** Under each frame, 16 lists of 400 tokens at temperature 1. From the parsed names: the distinct share, the junk share (names not among the {{def:CENSUS_SURNAMES|count}} Census surnames) and the mean −log p_Census(name) of generated in-Census names, against the Census self-entropy of {{num:reference.census_self_entropy_all|nats1}}. A model that only produces the most common names scores far below the self-entropy; a model that invents names shows a high junk share.\n    96\t\n    97\tThe surname test is shown, not scored.\n    98\t\n    99\t{{explainer:surnames}}\n   100\t\n   101\t## Tail statistics {#tail}\n   102\t\n   103\tFor each teacher-forced corpus (`tf`, `tf_fresh`, `tf_weird`) `vh_tail.py` records how the real text falls in the model's tail: the share of real tokens with s > 7 and s > 10 nats, the share with rank > 100 and rank > 1000, mean cross-entropy, mean entropy, the 99th percentile of s and the gap at positions with H > 4. A token at s > 10 is one the model gave less than about 1 in 22,000; on post-cutoff text these are real tokens the model treated as near-impossible. Shown, not scored.\n   104\t\n   105\t## Own-sample lexical statistics {#lexical}\n   106\t\n   107\t`vh_selfboot.py` takes the first 700 words of each bare-`<bos>` sample (samples with fewer than 60% of that budget are dropped) and computes type-token ratio, hapax share, distinct 4-gram share, Zipf slope and gzip ratio, against the same statistics on Pile documents at the same budget (the reference values ship in `reference.pile_lexical`). Free-running samples at T = 1 can fall into single-glyph or short-loop attractors; these statistics show it. They are shown and not scored, because the collapse is not diagnostic: pre-anneal and annealed checkpoints both do it (see [Findings](/findings#f4)).\n   108\t\n   109\t## Four ramps, one score {#score}\n   110\t\n   111\tEach sub-score is a clipped linear ramp. With feature value x, true-base anchor a (scores 1) and post-trained anchor b (scores 0):\n   112\t\n   113\t> sub-score = clip((x − b) / (a − b), 0, 1)\n   114\t\n   115\tThe anchors, features and fallbacks:\n   116\t\n   117\t{{block:anchors}}\n   118\t\n   119\tFor calib, a positive gap is clipped to 0 before the ramp, so under-confidence is not rewarded beyond a perfect score. The anchors were set from the Trinity TrueBase / Base / Preview triple and checked against the OLMo-2 ladder.\n   120\t\n   121\t**Overall = the mean of the available sub-scores.** A sub-score is unavailable when its feature and its fallback are both missing; the leaderboard shows how many sub-scores went into each overall score. A score of 1 means \"indistinguishable from a pre-anneal checkpoint on these four axes\". It is a distance, not a quality ranking: higher is not better.\n   122\t\n   123\t{{explainer:score}}\n   124\t\n   125\t## Limits {#limits}\n   126\t\n   127\t- The anchors come from two families. A recipe that changes the distribution in a way none of the four features sees will score as a true base.\n   128\t- `prior` rests on 32 samples and a marker list; a model that has seen instruction data but never emits its markers unprompted is not caught by it.\n   129\t- `memo` depends on Wikipedia being in the anneal mix. An anneal on other sources can memorise without moving it; the per-genre verbatim shares on `tf_weird` are there to check.\n   130\t- The post-cutoff corpus is post-cutoff for every model measured so far. A checkpoint trained on data from after April 2026 needs a newer corpus, and the bundle would need a new version.\n     1\t# Findings\n     2\t\n     3\tSix results from the current bundle. Each is stated as a claim whose numbers are computed from the bundle, followed by discussion and the charts that support it. Every number links to the field it came from; when a later bundle stops supporting a claim, a Data note appears under it instead of the old wording silently standing.\n     4\t\n     5\tStages follow the bundle's `stage` field: **pre-anneal** (a checkpoint from the constant-learning-rate phase of pretraining), **anneal** (during or after the learning-rate anneal and mid-training mix), **base** (the released base model, whatever its recipe) and **post-trained** (SFT, preference optimisation, RL).\n     6\t\n     7\t## Calibration ladder {#f1}\n     8\t\n     9\t{{block:f1-claim}}\n    10\t\n    11\tOn post-cutoff text a pre-anneal checkpoint expects as much surprise as it gets, overall and in every UncheatableEval domain. Each later stage moves the gap negative. The anneal itself barely moves the specimen ladder; SFT moves it far more than the anneal did, and DPO and RLVR push it further still. A negative gap of this size means the model's real surprisal on fresh text exceeds its entropy by most of a nat per token: it is confidently wrong far more often than its distribution admits.\n    12\t\n    13\t{{block:f1-detail}}\n    14\t\n    15\t{{block:ladder-calib}}\n    16\t\n    17\tIn the other families with both a base and a post-trained rung, the post-trained rung has the more negative gap; the small multiples show each family on a shared axis.\n    18\t\n    19\t{{block:ladder-calib-families}}\n    20\t\n    21\t{{block:f1-readoff}}\n    22\t\n    23\t## Annealed is not one thing {#f2}\n    24\t\n    25\t{{block:f2-claim}}\n    26\t\n    27\tThe two families with a measured pre-anneal checkpoint were annealed differently, and the sub-scores separate the recipes. One anneal recited its up-weighted sources while its prior stayed clean; the other kept its calibration and did not memorise Wikipedia, but its prior became instruction-shaped. A single \"base-ness\" number would put the two released bases near each other; the profiles show that they moved along different axes.\n    28\t\n    29\t{{block:f2-detail}}\n    30\t\n    31\t{{block:anneal-profiles}}\n    32\t\n    33\tAcross all anneal-stage and base checkpoints, the matrix of sub-scores shows which axis each recipe moved.\n    34\t\n    35\t{{block:subscore-matrix}}\n    36\t\n    37\t{{block:f2-readoff}}\n    38\t\n    39\t## Distillation {#f3}\n    40\t\n    41\t{{block:f3-claim}}\n    42\t\n    43\tA model released as a base can still have been trained toward a teacher's distribution rather than toward the data. On the calibration axis such a student sits with post-trained models, not with bases, and its gap where it is uncertain (H > 4) is nearly as far from zero as its instruction-tuned sibling's. Its prior, by contrast, is clean, and so is its Wikipedia recitation: training on a teacher's distribution transfers the teacher's sharpness without any instruction data.\n    44\t\n    45\t{{block:f3-detail}}\n    46\t\n    47\tThe scatter of calibration gap against marker rate shows the two axes separating. Post-training moves a model left (a more negative gap) and up (more instruction markers); distillation moves the student left only, and its instruction-tuned sibling then moves further left and up.\n    48\t\n    49\t{{block:gap-prior}}\n    50\t\n    51\t{{block:f3-readoff}}\n    52\t\n    53\t## Collapse {#f4}\n    54\t\n    55\t{{block:f4-claim}}\n    56\t\n    57\tSampled at temperature 1 with no truncation, many checkpoints eventually fall into attractors: single-glyph repetition or short loops where entropy drops near zero and stays there. It is tempting to read this as a post-training artefact. It is not: pre-anneal and annealed checkpoints do it as readily as post-trained ones. Collapse is shown on model pages but is not part of the score.\n    58\t\n    59\t{{block:collapse-strip}}\n    60\t\n    61\t{{block:f4-readoff}}\n    62\t\n    63\t## Surnames {#f5}\n    64\t\n    65\t{{block:f5-claim}}\n    66\t\n    67\tAsked in words for a random list of US surnames, every model in the bench answers with the genre \"ranked list of common surnames\": usually Smith first, then Johnson, Williams, and so on down the Census frequency table. This is a correct reading of what such lists look like on the web, not a sample from the population. Post-training sharpens it into near-verbatim recitation of the Census ranking.\n    68\t\n    69\tGiven six genuine Census draws as items 1 to 6, the picture separates. Pre-anneal checkpoints and clean bases match the population distribution at item 7 closely, against a Census entropy of {{num:reference.census_entropy_top2000|nats}} over the same 2,000 names. Post-training thins the tail.\n    70\t\n    71\t{{block:f5-detail}}\n    72\t\n    73\t{{block:surname-kl}}\n    74\t\n    75\tAlong the specimen ladder the effective number of surnames, exp(H) over the few-shot frames, changes little through the anneal and collapses with post-training; the dashed line is the Census over the same names.\n    76\t\n    77\t{{block:ladder-surname-kl}}\n    78\t\n    79\t{{block:f5-readoff}}\n    80\t\n    81\t## Tail {#f6}\n    82\t\n    83\t{{block:f6-claim}}\n    84\t\n    85\tOn post-cutoff text, post-training raises the share of real tokens the model found near-impossible: surprisal above 10 nats, a probability below about 1 in 22,000. The paired chart below shows every base / post-trained pair, including the ones where the rise is small.\n    86\t\n    87\tThe over-confidence concentrates where the model is uncertain. At positions with H > 4 nats, bases have a gap near zero, while most post-trained models are well below it; the exceptions are named in the claim above and visible in the strip. Post-training does not just sharpen confident predictions; it removes the hedging from uncertain ones.\n    88\t\n    89\t{{block:f6-detail}}\n    90\t\n    91\t{{block:tail-arrows}}\n    92\t\n    93\t{{block:tail-strip}}\n    94\t\n    95\t{{block:f6-readoff}}\n    96\t\n    97\t## Every family, in stage order {#ladders}\n    98\t\n    99\tEach family's rungs in stage order, for a selectable metric (calibration gap, few-shot surname KL, top-1 ECE), on a shared axis.\n   100\t\n   101\t{{block:ladders}}\n     1\t# Measure your model\n     2\t\n     3\tEvery checkpoint on the board was measured with the scripts in `docs/` of this repository, on the same fixed text. To add one, run the same passes on your checkpoint, run the analysis scripts, export a bundle with `tools/export_bundle.py` and open a pull request. This page walks through each step.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Environment {#environment}\n     6\t\n     7\t- **Hardware.** The reference machine is one node with 8×B200 (about 180 GB each). Smaller checkpoints run on fewer or smaller GPUs; the HF path spreads layers over every visible GPU with `device_map=\"auto\"`, and the vLLM path uses tensor parallelism.\n     8\t- **Python.** 3.10 or newer with `torch`, `transformers`, `numpy` and `datasets` (the HF path), plus `vllm` with the V1 engine for the vLLM path. The analysis scripts need only `numpy`.\n     9\t- **Precision.** Weights load in bf16 unless you ask otherwise; all distribution statistics are computed in float32 from the full logits row. Record any change (FP8 quantisation, dequantisation) in your submission.\n    10\t- **Paths.** The script defaults point at `/models/varentropy/...` on the original machine. Pass `--corpus` and `--out` explicitly. Two analysis scripts still have the path in their source: `vh_selfboot.py` (`corpus_path`) and `vh_tail.py` (`res`). Edit those two lines to point at your corpus file and results directory.\n    11\t\n    12\t## Corpora {#corpora}\n    13\t\n    14\tEvery model must read exactly the same documents, so download the published corpus files rather than rebuilding them. They live next to the data bundles, under `corpus/` in the bench's data bucket, with a `SHA256SUMS` file; check your copies against it before measuring. All four teacher-forced corpora are JSON files of the form `{\"tf\": [{\"idx\", \"set\", \"text\"}, ...], \"openers\": [...]}`.\n    15\t\n    16\t| file (flag) | pass | contents |\n    17\t|---|---|---|\n    18\t| `pile10k_sel.json` (`--corpus`) | `tf`, own-sample openers | 400 Pile documents plus 64 openers, a fixed seeded selection from NeelNanda/pile-10k |\n    19\t| `wiki_gh.json` (`--extra-corpus tf_wiki=PATH`) | `tf_wiki` | 150 Wikipedia and 60 GitHub documents |\n    20\t| `uncheatable_2026_04.json` (`--extra-corpus tf_fresh=PATH`) | `tf_fresh` | 900 post-cutoff documents in 15 categories (UncheatableEval 2026-04) |\n    21\t| `weird.json` (`--extra-corpus tf_weird=PATH`) | `tf_weird` | 199 documents from 12 rare Pile genres |\n    22\t| `names.json` (`--names-probe`) | surname probe | `{\"surnames\": [[name, count], ...]}` from the 2010 US Census, most common first |\n    23\t\n    24\t{{block:corpora}}\n    25\t\n    26\tIf `--corpus` points at a file that does not exist, `vh_score.py` builds the Pile selection from the pile-10k Arrow file given by `--arrow` (seed 0, documents of at least 400 characters) and writes it there. Only do this to reproduce the published file; a different selection makes your numbers incomparable.\n    27\t\n    28\tThe `tf_fresh` texts post-date the training cutoff of every model in v1. If your checkpoint's data extends past April 2026, its `tf_fresh` pass is no longer post-cutoff; say so in the pull request.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Run the passes: HF transformers {#run-hf}\n    31\t\n    32\tUse `vh_score.py` for dense checkpoints and MoE checkpoints that transformers loads natively.\n    33\t\n    34\t```bash\n    35\tpython docs/vh_score.py --model /path/to/checkpoint --name my-model \\\n    36\t  --out results --corpus corpus/pile10k_sel.json \\\n    37\t  --extra-corpus tf_wiki=corpus/wiki_gh.json \\\n    38\t  --extra-corpus tf_fresh=corpus/uncheatable_2026_04.json \\\n    39\t  --extra-corpus tf_weird=corpus/weird.json \\\n    40\t  --names-probe corpus/names.json --max-mem-gib 165\n    41\t```\n    42\t\n    43\tUseful flags:\n    44\t\n    45\t- `--max-mem-gib` caps the memory per GPU used for weights, leaving room for full-vocabulary logits.\n    46\t- `--trust-remote-code` for custom-code checkpoints; `--arch deepseek_v3` forces the native DeepSeek-V3 class for Kimi K2 and DeepSeek-V3-architecture checkpoints that ship custom code.\n    47\t- `--quantize-fp8` quantises a bf16 checkpoint to block-128 FP8 at load; `--fp8-dequantize` does the reverse for FP8 checkpoints the quantiser cannot wrap.\n    48\t- `--experts-impl` selects the transformers experts implementation for MoE models.\n    49\t- `--skip-tf` / `--skip-rollouts` rerun only part of a model. Extra corpora whose `.npz` already exists are skipped, so an interrupted run can be resumed.\n    50\t\n    51\t## Run the passes: vLLM {#run-vllm}\n    52\t\n    53\tUse `vh_score_vllm.py` for checkpoints transformers cannot load efficiently, such as FP8 custom-code MoE. It writes the same files as the HF path. Exact per-step statistics come from the logits processor in `vh_lp.py`; teacher forcing replays each document with the real token forced through the logits mask. vLLM loads the processor by module name in its worker processes, so put `docs/` on `PYTHONPATH`.\n    54\t\n    55\t```bash\n    56\tPYTHONPATH=docs python docs/vh_score_vllm.py --model /path/to/checkpoint --name my-model \\\n    57\t  --out results --corpus corpus/pile10k_sel.json --tp 8 --trust-remote-code \\\n    58\t  --extra-corpus tf_wiki=corpus/wiki_gh.json \\\n    59\t  --extra-corpus tf_fresh=corpus/uncheatable_2026_04.json \\\n    60\t  --extra-corpus tf_weird=corpus/weird.json \\\n    61\t  --names-probe corpus/names.json --rollout-steps 1024\n    62\t```\n    63\t\n    64\t- `--prepend-bos` adds the BOS token when the tokenizer does not (DeepSeek V4 needs it). Check that your tokenizer's first token on the corpus is BOS on either path.\n    65\t- `--rollout-steps 1024` is what the FP8 giants used in v1; the default and the reference value elsewhere is 1536. A shorter rollout lowers the collapse statistics' exposure and must be declared.\n    66\t- `--gpu-mem`, `--max-num-seqs`, `--max-model-len` and `--max-num-batched-tokens` are passed through to vLLM.\n    67\t- Prefix caching is disabled on purpose: each request must produce its own statistics.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Expected runtime {#runtime}\n    70\t\n    71\tThe work is fixed: about 290k Pile tokens, 1.3M post-cutoff tokens, the Wikipedia/GitHub and rare-genre sets, 64 sampled sequences of 1536 steps, 6 × 2,000 surname scorings and 6 × 16 sampled surname lists. Every position keeps the full vocabulary distribution, so throughput is lower than for an ordinary evaluation run. Runtime therefore scales with parameter count and vocabulary size: on the reference node, expect minutes to tens of minutes per pass for a small dense model and hours in total for the largest MoE checkpoints. On the vLLM path teacher forcing costs more than a single forward pass per document, because each document is replayed step by step. The load time is written to `run.json` in the result directory, and every phase logs its own timing.\n    72\t\n    73\t## Analyse {#analyse}\n    74\t\n    75\tRun in this order, from the repository root. Each script reads the per-model directories under `results/` and writes into `results/analysis/`.\n    76\t\n    77\t```bash\n    78\tpython docs/vh_analyze.py results --all --corpus corpus/pile10k_sel.json   # fingerprints.json\n    79\tpython docs/vh_memo.py results my-model                                    # memo_<model>.json\n    80\tpython docs/vh_selfboot.py results                                         # selfboot.json\n    81\tpython docs/vh_tail.py                                                     # tail_realised.json\n    82\tpython docs/vh_names_table.py results                                      # names_summary.json, weird_summary.json\n    83\tpython docs/vh_truebase.py results/analysis/fingerprints.json              # truebase_scores.json\n    84\t```\n    85\t\n    86\t`vh_truebase.py` prints the four sub-scores and the overall score; these are the numbers the leaderboard will show. `vh_report.py` renders a standalone HTML report and is not needed for a submission.\n    87\t\n    88\t## Export {#export}\n    89\t\n    90\tFirst add your checkpoint to the model registry `tools/models.json`, keyed by its result-directory name: its `family`, `stage` (one of pre-anneal, anneal, base, post-trained), a short `label` and its `order` in the family ladder. Models that are not in the registry are skipped by the export (`--strict` makes that an error). Then export and test:\n    91\t\n    92\t```bash\n    93\tpython tools/export_bundle.py results/analysis --out data/\n    94\tcd site && npm test\n    95\t```\n    96\t\n    97\t`export_bundle.py` merges the analysis files into one bundle in the schema below, carries over every model of the latest bundle that you did not re-measure, writes it as `bench_vN.json` and updates `manifest.json`. Use `--dry-run` first to see the summary without writing anything. `npm test` checks the bundle against the data contract and recomputes every overall score from the fingerprint with the published ramps; a mismatch fails the test. To preview, run `npm run dev` in `site/` and open your model's page.\n    98\t\n    99\t## Submit {#submit}\n   100\t\n   101\tOpen a pull request that adds your entries to a new `bench_vN.json` and lists it in `data/manifest.json`. Do not edit a published bundle; bundles are append-only so that every number ever shown on the site stays reproducible. Include in the pull request description:\n   102\t\n   103\t- the checkpoint id (a Hugging Face repo id and revision, or a public download location), its family, stage and position in the family ladder;\n   104\t- the commit of this repository whose scripts you ran, and the exact command lines;\n   105\t- hardware, precision, and every flag that differs from the defaults (FP8, rollout steps, `--prepend-bos`);\n   106\t- the output of `vh_truebase.py` for your model;\n   107\t- a passing `npm test`.\n   108\t\n   109\tMaintainers may rerun some passes on publicly downloadable checkpoints before merging, and will ask in the pull request if a number does not reproduce.\n   110\t\n   111\t## Data contract {#contract}\n   112\t\n   113\t{{block:datacontract}}\n     1\t# About\n     2\t\n     3\tTrue Base Bench is a public, permanent benchmark that measures how far a language-model checkpoint sits from a pre-anneal pretraining base: a checkpoint from the long constant-learning-rate phase of pretraining, before the anneal, mid-training mixes and any post-training. It is built and maintained by [Anima Labs](https://github.com/anima-research).\n     4\t\n     5\t## What it is for {#purpose}\n     6\t\n     7\t\"Base model\" has stopped meaning one thing. Released bases are often annealed on curated, instruction-heavy or synthetic mixes, distilled from a larger model, or both. For research that depends on a model trained toward the data and nothing else (interpretability, studies of the pretraining distribution, simulation, uses of base-model sampling that rely on its full diversity), the label on the checkpoint is not enough.\n     8\t\n     9\tThe bench gives that question a measurement. It does not score capability, and a higher score is not better in general: a score of 1 means the checkpoint is indistinguishable from a pre-anneal base on the four features measured, nothing more. Whether that is what you want depends on what you are using the model for.\n    10\t\n    11\t## How it works, briefly {#how}\n    12\t\n    13\tEvery model reads the same fixed text, and every feature is a statistic of its own next-token distribution: how well its expected surprise matches its realised surprise on text written after its training cutoff, how much of Wikipedia it recites verbatim, and how often it writes chat or instruction markers when asked to write from nothing. Four sub-scores, each a clipped linear ramp between a true-base anchor and a post-trained anchor, are averaged into the overall score. A surname-distribution test, tail statistics and lexical statistics of the model's own samples are shown alongside. [Methodology](/methodology) defines each of these precisely; [Findings](/findings) gives the results.\n    14\t\n    15\t## Data and reproducibility {#data}\n    16\t\n    17\t- All numbers on the site are read from a versioned data bundle (`bench_vN.json`, listed in `manifest.json`), served from public object storage with a copy in the repository. No number is typed into a page.\n    18\t- The measurement and analysis scripts are in `docs/` in the repository, unchanged from the runs that produced the bundle. [Measure your model](/measure) gives the exact commands.\n    19\t- The overall score is recomputable from the fingerprint in the bundle; the site's test suite checks this for every model.\n    20\t- Bundles are append-only. A new measurement, a new model or a change of method produces a new bundle version; earlier versions stay available so that cited numbers remain checkable.\n    21\t\n    22\t## Maintenance {#maintenance}\n    23\t\n    24\tThe bench is maintained by Anima Labs. New checkpoints are added through pull requests that include a bundle produced with the published scripts (see [Measure your model](/measure)), and by the maintainers as notable checkpoints are released. Changes to the method, such as new anchors, a new post-cutoff corpus or a new feature, are made only in a new bundle version, described in the pull request that introduces it; a model's score is always reported against a named bundle version.\n    25\t\n    26\tThe post-cutoff corpus has a date. Once checkpoints trained on data from after that date appear, the corpus has to be replaced by a newer one, in a new bundle version, with the existing models re-measured on it.\n    27\t\n    28\tCorrections, questions and submissions go to the issue tracker of the repository at [github.com/anima-research](https://github.com/anima-research).\n    29\t\n    30\t## Citation {#citation}\n    31\t\n    32\tIf you use the bench or its data, cite the bundle version you used. The entry below is for the bundle this page loaded, {{val:bundle}}; earlier versions stay listed in `manifest.json`.\n    33\t\n    34\t{{block:citation}}\n    35\t\n    36\t## Acknowledgements {#acknowledgements}\n    37\t\n    38\tThe surname test follows a proposal by Gwern Branwen. The post-cutoff text comes from UncheatableEval, the Pile selection from NeelNanda/pile-10k, and the surname reference from the 2010 US Census surname file. The anchors were set using checkpoints released by Arcee AI (Trinity) and the Allen Institute for AI (OLMo-2), whose publication of intermediate checkpoints makes a measurement like this possible.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba site/src/findings.ts && nl -ba site/src/App.tsx && nl -ba site/src/main.tsx && nl -ba site/src/data/BundleContext.tsx'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba site/src/findings.ts && nl -ba site/src/App.tsx && nl -ba site/src/main.tsx && nl -ba site/src/data/BundleContext.tsx'","aggregated_output":"     1\t/**\n     2\t * Findings F1–F6 (DESIGN §7.4): every claim is computed from the bundle and has a predicate.\n     3\t * If a predicate fails, the page shows a Data note saying what no longer holds. Pure functions: tested in\n     4\t * src/test/findings.test.ts (snapshot of computed ranges).\n     5\t */\n     6\timport { fp, get } from \"./data/bundle\";\n     7\timport { basePairFor, collapseSpecimen, ladder, median, memoTriple, memoValue, preAnnealBand, specimenFamily, specimenPair } from \"./data/derive\";\n     8\timport { SURNAME_FRAMES } from \"./data/method\";\n     9\timport type { Bundle, Model } from \"./data/types\";\n    10\t\n    11\t/** Definitions used by the checks (not measurements). */\n    12\texport const FINDING_DEFS = {\n    13\t  /** SPEC: pre-anneal checkpoints sit within ±0.02 of 0 */\n    14\t  PRE_BAND_MAX: 0.02,\n    15\t  /** tolerance for non-monotone steps inside one stage (OLMo-2 anneal rungs) */\n    16\t  STAGE_TOL: 0.005,\n    17\t  /** a base counts as \"clean\" for the surname range when its overall score is at least this */\n    18\t  CLEAN_BASE_MIN_OVERALL: 0.85,\n    19\t  /** a post-trained rung supports F6 (gap) when gap_hiH falls by more than this vs its base pair, nats */\n    20\t  F6_GAP_SUPPORT: 0.25,\n    21\t  /** a post-trained rung supports F6 (rate) when the s>10 rate rises by at least this fraction */\n    22\t  F6_RATE_SUPPORT: 0.25,\n    23\t  /** a base counts as ≈ 0 on gap_hiH within this many nats */\n    24\t  BASE_HIH_ZERO: 0.15,\n    25\t};\n    26\t\n    27\texport interface Check {\n    28\t  ok: boolean;\n    29\t  /** what no longer holds, when !ok */\n    30\t  note: string;\n    31\t}\n    32\t\n    33\tconst num = (x: number | null | undefined): x is number => typeof x === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(x);\n    34\t\n    35\t// ---------- F1 calibration ladder ----------\n    36\texport interface F1 {\n    37\t  band: { band: number; model: Model } | null;\n    38\t  family: string | null;\n    39\t  /** last rung of each stage, post-trained rungs individually, in order */\n    40\t  steps: Model[];\n    41\t  checks: { band: Check; monotone: Check; domains: Check };\n    42\t  preDomainMin: { model: Model; value: number }[];\n    43\t}\n    44\t\n    45\t/** Last rung of each pre/anneal/base stage, plus every post-trained rung (SFT → DPO → RLVR are separate steps). */\n    46\texport function stageSteps(rungs: Model[]): Model[] {\n    47\t  const out: Model[] = [];\n    48\t  rungs.forEach((r, i) => {\n    49\t    const next = rungs[i + 1];\n    50\t    if (r.stage === \"post-trained\" || !next || next.stage !== r.stage) out.push(r);\n    51\t  });\n    52\t  return out;\n    53\t}\n    54\t\n    55\texport function f1(b: Bundle): F1 {\n    56\t  const band = preAnnealBand(b);\n    57\t  const family = specimenFamily(b);\n    58\t  const rungs = family ? ladder(b, family) : [];\n    59\t  const steps = stageSteps(rungs);\n    60\t  const g = (m: Model) => fp(m, \"F_calib_gap\");\n    61\t  const bandOk = !!band && band.band <= FINDING_DEFS.PRE_BAND_MAX;\n    62\t  // monotone across stage steps; inside a stage allow STAGE_TOL rises\n    63\t  const bad: string[] = [];\n    64\t  for (let i = 1; i < steps.length; i++) {\n    65\t    const a = g(steps[i - 1]);\n    66\t    const c = g(steps[i]);\n    67\t    if (num(a) && num(c) && c > a) bad.push(`${steps[i - 1].label} → ${steps[i].label}`);\n    68\t  }\n    69\t  for (let i = 1; i < rungs.length; i++) {\n    70\t    if (rungs[i].stage !== rungs[i - 1].stage || rungs[i].stage === \"post-trained\") continue;\n    71\t    const a = g(rungs[i - 1]);\n    72\t    const c = g(rungs[i]);\n    73\t    if (num(a) && num(c) && c - a > FINDING_DEFS.STAGE_TOL) bad.push(`${rungs[i - 1].label} → ${rungs[i].label} (inside a stage)`);\n    74\t  }\n    75\t  const pres = b.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"pre-anneal\");\n    76\t  const preDomainMin = pres.map((m) => ({ model: m, value: fp(m, \"F_gap_domain_min\") })).filter((x): x is { model: Model; value: number } => num(x.value));\n    77\t  const domBad = preDomainMin.filter((x) => x.value < -FINDING_DEFS.PRE_BAND_MAX);\n    78\t  return {\n    79\t    band,\n    80\t    family,\n    81\t    steps,\n    82\t    preDomainMin,\n    83\t    checks: {\n    84\t      band: {\n    85\t        ok: bandOk,\n    86\t        note: band\n    87\t          ? `the widest pre-anneal gap (${band.model.label}) exceeds ±${FINDING_DEFS.PRE_BAND_MAX} nats`\n    88\t          : \"no pre-anneal checkpoint with a post-cutoff gap in this bundle\",\n    89\t      },\n    90\t      monotone: {\n    91\t        ok: steps.length > 1 && bad.length === 0,\n    92\t        note: steps.length > 1 ? `the gap does not fall monotonically along ${family}: ${bad.join(\"; \")}` : \"no ladder with both pre-anneal and post-trained rungs\",\n    93\t      },\n    94\t      domains: {\n    95\t        ok: domBad.length === 0,\n    96\t        note: `a pre-anneal checkpoint has a post-cutoff domain gap below −${FINDING_DEFS.PRE_BAND_MAX}: ${domBad.map((x) => x.model.label).join(\", \")}`,\n    97\t      },\n    98\t    },\n    99\t  };\n   100\t}\n   101\t\n   102\t// ---------- F2 annealed is not one thing ----------\n   103\texport interface F2 {\n   104\t  memo: Model[] | null; // last pre-anneal, first annealed, next post\n   105\t  priorFamily: string | null;\n   106\t  priorPre: Model | null;\n   107\t  priorAnneal: Model[];\n   108\t  priorRange: [number, number] | null;\n   109\t  checks: { memo: Check; prior: Check; different: Check };\n   110\t}\n   111\t\n   112\texport function f2(b: Bundle): F2 {\n   113\t  const memo = memoTriple(b);\n   114\t  const fam = specimenFamily(b);\n   115\t  const rungs = fam ? ladder(b, fam) : [];\n   116\t  const pre = rungs.find((r) => r.stage === \"pre-anneal\") ?? null;\n   117\t  const ann = rungs.filter((r) => r.stage === \"anneal\" || r.stage === \"base\");\n   118\t  const rates = ann.map((r) => fp(r, \"roll_instr_marker_rate\")).filter(num);\n   119\t  const priorRange: [number, number] | null = rates.length ? [Math.min(...rates), Math.max(...rates)] : null;\n   120\t  const memoRise = memo && memo.length >= 2 ? (memoValue(memo[1]) ?? 0) - (memoValue(memo[0]) ?? 0) : 0;\n   121\t  const memoPriorShift = memo && memo.length >= 2 ? Math.abs((fp(memo[1], \"roll_instr_marker_rate\") ?? 0) - (fp(memo[0], \"roll_instr_marker_rate\") ?? 0)) : 1;\n   122\t  const preRate = pre ? fp(pre, \"roll_instr_marker_rate\") : null;\n   123\t  const priorRise = priorRange && num(preRate) ? priorRange[0] - preRate : 0;\n   124\t  const memoShiftPriorFam = pre && ann.length ? Math.max(...ann.map((r) => Math.abs((memoValue(r) ?? 0) - (memoValue(pre) ?? 0)))) : 1;\n   125\t  return {\n   126\t    memo,\n   127\t    priorFamily: fam,\n   128\t    priorPre: pre,\n   129\t    priorAnneal: ann,\n   130\t    priorRange,\n   131\t    checks: {\n   132\t      memo: { ok: memoRise > 0.05, note: \"no family’s anneal raises the Wikipedia verbatim share by more than 5 points\" },\n   133\t      prior: { ok: priorRise > 0.1, note: `the ${fam ?? \"specimen\"} anneal does not raise the instruction-marker rate by more than 10 points on every annealed rung` },\n   134\t      different: {\n   135\t        ok: memoPriorShift < 0.05 && memoShiftPriorFam < 0.02,\n   136\t        note: \"the two anneals no longer move different sub-scores (each moved the other’s feature too)\",\n   137\t      },\n   138\t    },\n   139\t  };\n   140\t}\n   141\t\n   142\t// ---------- F3 distillation ----------\n   143\texport interface F3 {\n   144\t  distilled: Model[];\n   145\t  student: Model | null;\n   146\t  studentPost: Model | null;\n   147\t  otherBaseMin: { model: Model; value: number } | null;\n   148\t  postMax: { model: Model; value: number } | null;\n   149\t  checks: { looksPost: Check };\n   150\t}\n   151\t\n   152\texport function f3(b: Bundle): F3 {\n   153\t  const distilled = b.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"base\" && /distill/i.test(m.label));\n   154\t  const student = distilled[0] ?? null;\n   155\t  const g = (m: Model) => fp(m, \"F_calib_gap\");\n   156\t  const others = b.models.filter((m) => (m.stage === \"base\" || m.stage === \"anneal\") && !distilled.includes(m) && num(g(m)));\n   157\t  others.sort((x, y) => g(x)! - g(y)!);\n   158\t  const posts = b.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"post-trained\" && num(g(m))).sort((x, y) => g(y)! - g(x)!);\n   159\t  const studentPost = student ? ladder(b, student.family).find((r) => r.order > student.order && r.stage === \"post-trained\") ?? null : null;\n   160\t  const otherBaseMin = others[0] ? { model: others[0], value: g(others[0])! } : null;\n   161\t  const postMax = posts[0] ? { model: posts[0], value: g(posts[0])! } : null;\n   162\t  const sg = student ? g(student) : null;\n   163\t  const ok = num(sg) && !!otherBaseMin && !!postMax && sg < otherBaseMin.value && sg <= postMax.value;\n   164\t  return {\n   165\t    distilled,\n   166\t    student,\n   167\t    studentPost,\n   168\t    otherBaseMin,\n   169\t    postMax,\n   170\t    checks: {\n   171\t      looksPost: {\n   172\t        ok,\n   173\t        note: student\n   174\t          ? `${student.label} (${student.family}) is no longer below every other base and inside the post-trained range on F_calib_gap`\n   175\t          : \"no base checkpoint labelled as distilled in this bundle\",\n   176\t      },\n   177\t    },\n   178\t  };\n   179\t}\n   180\t\n   181\t// ---------- F4 collapse ----------\n   182\texport interface F4 {\n   183\t  specimen: Model | null;\n   184\t  family: Model[];\n   185\t  postRepMedian: number | null;\n   186\t  checks: { notDiagnostic: Check };\n   187\t}\n   188\t\n   189\texport function f4(b: Bundle): F4 {\n   190\t  const sp = collapseSpecimen(b);\n   191\t  const fam = sp ? ladder(b, sp.family) : [];\n   192\t  const postRep = b.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"post-trained\").map((m) => fp(m, \"roll_rep8_q4\")).filter(num);\n   193\t  const postRepMedian = median(postRep);\n   194\t  const nextAnnealed = sp ? fam.find((r) => r.order > sp.order && r.stage !== \"post-trained\") : undefined;\n   195\t  const r = (m?: Model) => (m ? fp(m, \"roll_rep8_q4\") : null);\n   196\t  const ok = num(postRepMedian) && num(r(sp ?? undefined)) && r(sp!)! >= postRepMedian && (!nextAnnealed || (num(r(nextAnnealed)) && r(nextAnnealed)! >= postRepMedian));\n   197\t  return {\n   198\t    specimen: sp,\n   199\t    family: fam,\n   200\t    postRepMedian,\n   201\t    checks: {\n   202\t      notDiagnostic: {\n   203\t        ok,\n   204\t        note: \"the pre-anneal and annealed collapse specimens no longer repeat at least as much as the median post-trained checkpoint\",\n   205\t      },\n   206\t    },\n   207\t  };\n   208\t}\n   209\t\n   210\t// ---------- F5 surnames ----------\n   211\texport interface F5 {\n   212\t  cleanRange: [number, number] | null;\n   213\t  clean: Model[];\n   214\t  B: Model | null;\n   215\t  allDescAboveCensus: boolean;\n   216\t  nWithSurnames: number;\n   217\t  sharpenPairs: { base: Model; post: Model }[];\n   218\t  checks: { genre: Check; clean: Check; thinned: Check };\n   219\t}\n   220\t\n   221\texport function f5(b: Bundle): F5 {\n   222\t  const ms = b.models.filter((m) => m.surnames && num(m.surnames.KL_few));\n   223\t  const clean = ms.filter((m) => m.stage === \"pre-anneal\" || (m.stage === \"base\" && (m.score ?? 0) >= FINDING_DEFS.CLEAN_BASE_MIN_OVERALL));\n   224\t  const kl = clean.map((m) => m.surnames!.KL_few as number);\n   225\t  const cleanRange: [number, number] | null = kl.length ? [Math.min(...kl), Math.max(...kl)] : null;\n   226\t  const B = specimenPair(b)?.B ?? null;\n   227\t  const descOk = b.models.filter((m) => m.surnames && num(m.surnames.top10_desc) && num(m.surnames.top10_r));\n   228\t  const allDescAboveCensus = descOk.length > 0 && descOk.every((m) => (m.surnames!.top10_desc as number) > (m.surnames!.top10_r as number));\n   229\t  const pairs = b.models\n   230\t    .filter((m) => m.stage === \"post-trained\" && m.surnames)\n   231\t    .map((p) => ({ post: p, base: basePairFor(b, p) }))\n   232\t    .filter((x): x is { base: Model; post: Model } => !!x.base && !!x.base.surnames);\n   233\t  const sharpen = pairs.filter((x) => num(x.post.surnames!.top10_desc) && num(x.base.surnames!.top10_desc) && x.post.surnames!.top10_desc! > x.base.surnames!.top10_desc!);\n   234\t  const bKL = B?.surnames?.KL_few;\n   235\t  return {\n   236\t    cleanRange,\n   237\t    clean,\n   238\t    B,\n   239\t    allDescAboveCensus,\n   240\t    nWithSurnames: ms.length,\n   241\t    sharpenPairs: sharpen,\n   242\t    checks: {\n   243\t      genre: { ok: allDescAboveCensus, note: \"some checkpoint’s described-frame top-10 mass is not above the Census top-10 share\" },\n   244\t      clean: { ok: !!cleanRange && cleanRange[1] < 0.5, note: \"a pre-anneal checkpoint or clean base has few-shot KL above 0.5\" },\n   245\t      thinned: {\n   246\t        ok: num(bKL) && !!cleanRange && bKL > 2 * cleanRange[1],\n   247\t        note: \"the specimen post-trained checkpoint’s few-shot KL is no longer more than twice the clean range\",\n   248\t      },\n   249\t    },\n   250\t  };\n   251\t}\n   252\t\n   253\texport const FEW_FRAMES = SURNAME_FRAMES.few;\n   254\t\n   255\t// ---------- F6 tail ----------\n   256\texport interface TailPair {\n   257\t  base: Model;\n   258\t  post: Model;\n   259\t  rateBase: number;\n   260\t  ratePost: number;\n   261\t  rise: number; // ratePost / rateBase − 1\n   262\t  hiBase: number | null;\n   263\t  hiPost: number | null;\n   264\t  hiDelta: number | null; // post − base\n   265\t}\n   266\t\n   267\texport interface F6 {\n   268\t  pairs: TailPair[];\n   269\t  rateSupport: TailPair[];\n   270\t  rateExceptions: TailPair[];\n   271\t  rateRange: [number, number] | null;\n   272\t  gapSupport: TailPair[];\n   273\t  gapExceptions: TailPair[];\n   274\t  gapRange: [number, number] | null; // magnitudes of the fall, nats\n   275\t  baseHiMedian: number | null;\n   276\t  baseHiExceptions: { model: Model; value: number }[];\n   277\t  checks: { rate: Check; gap: Check; bases: Check };\n   278\t}\n   279\t\n   280\texport function f6(b: Bundle): F6 {\n   281\t  const pairs: TailPair[] = [];\n   282\t  for (const post of b.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"post-trained\")) {\n   283\t    const base = basePairFor(b, post);\n   284\t    if (!base) continue;\n   285\t    const rb = get(base, [\"tail\", \"tf_fresh\", \"s>10\"]);\n   286\t    const rp = get(post, [\"tail\", \"tf_fresh\", \"s>10\"]);\n   287\t    if (!num(rb) || !num(rp) || rb <= 0) continue;\n   288\t    const hb = get(base, \"tail.tf_fresh.gap_hiH\");\n   289\t    const hp = get(post, \"tail.tf_fresh.gap_hiH\");\n   290\t    pairs.push({ base, post, rateBase: rb, ratePost: rp, rise: rp / rb - 1, hiBase: hb, hiPost: hp, hiDelta: num(hb) && num(hp) ? hp - hb : null });\n   291\t  }\n   292\t  const rateSupport = pairs.filter((p) => p.rise >= FINDING_DEFS.F6_RATE_SUPPORT);\n   293\t  const rateExceptions = pairs.filter((p) => p.rise < FINDING_DEFS.F6_RATE_SUPPORT);\n   294\t  const rr = rateSupport.map((p) => p.rise);\n   295\t  const gapSupport = pairs.filter((p) => num(p.hiDelta) && p.hiDelta < -FINDING_DEFS.F6_GAP_SUPPORT);\n   296\t  const gapExceptions = pairs.filter((p) => !(num(p.hiDelta) && p.hiDelta < -FINDING_DEFS.F6_GAP_SUPPORT));\n   297\t  const gr = gapSupport.map((p) => -p.hiDelta!);\n   298\t  const bases = b.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"base\" || m.stage === \"pre-anneal\" || m.stage === \"anneal\");\n   299\t  const bh = bases.map((m) => ({ model: m, value: get(m, \"tail.tf_fresh.gap_hiH\") })).filter((x): x is { model: Model; value: number } => num(x.value));\n   300\t  const baseHiExceptions = bh.filter((x) => Math.abs(x.value) > FINDING_DEFS.BASE_HIH_ZERO);\n   301\t  return {\n   302\t    pairs,\n   303\t    rateSupport,\n   304\t    rateExceptions,\n   305\t    rateRange: rr.length ? [Math.min(...rr), Math.max(...rr)] : null,\n   306\t    gapSupport,\n   307\t    gapExceptions,\n   308\t    gapRange: gr.length ? [Math.min(...gr), Math.max(...gr)] : null,\n   309\t    baseHiMedian: median(bh.map((x) => x.value)),\n   310\t    baseHiExceptions,\n   311\t    checks: {\n   312\t      rate: { ok: pairs.length > 0 && rateSupport.length >= pairs.length / 2, note: \"fewer than half of the base → post-trained pairs raise the s > 10 rate by 25% or more\" },\n   313\t      gap: { ok: pairs.length > 0 && gapSupport.length >= pairs.length / 2, note: \"fewer than half of the pairs become over-confident at H > 4 by more than 0.25 nats\" },\n   314\t      bases: {\n   315\t        ok: bh.length > 0 && baseHiExceptions.length <= Math.max(1, Math.floor(bh.length / 5)),\n   316\t        note: \"more than a fifth of pre-anneal, anneal and base checkpoints sit away from 0 at H > 4\",\n   317\t      },\n   318\t    },\n   319\t  };\n   320\t}\n   321\t\n   322\texport function allFindings(b: Bundle) {\n   323\t  return { f1: f1(b), f2: f2(b), f3: f3(b), f4: f4(b), f5: f5(b), f6: f6(b) };\n   324\t}\n     1\timport { lazy, Suspense, useEffect, type ReactNode } from \"react\";\n     2\timport { Route, Routes, useLocation } from \"react-router-dom\";\n     3\timport { BundleProvider, useBundleState } from \"./data/BundleContext\";\n     4\timport { MotionProvider } from \"./lib/motion\";\n     5\timport { Footer, TopBar } from \"./components/Chrome\";\n     6\timport { SvgDefs } from \"./components/Bars\";\n     7\timport Leaderboard from \"./pages/Leaderboard\";\n     8\t\n     9\tconst ModelPage = lazy(() => import(\"./pages/ModelPage\"));\n    10\tconst Findings = lazy(() => import(\"./pages/Findings\"));\n    11\tconst Methodology = lazy(() => import(\"./pages/Methodology\"));\n    12\tconst Measure = lazy(() => import(\"./pages/Measure\"));\n    13\tconst About = lazy(() => import(\"./pages/About\"));\n    14\tconst ExplainersReview = lazy(() => import(\"./pages/ExplainersReview\"));\n    15\t\n    16\tfunction Skeleton() {\n    17\t  return (\n    18\t    <div className=\"container\" aria-busy=\"true\" aria-label=\"Loading data\">\n    19\t      <div className=\"page-head\">\n    20\t        <div className=\"skeleton-row\" />\n    21\t      </div>\n    22\t      {Array.from({ length: 10 }, (_, i) => (\n    23\t        <div key={i} className=\"skeleton-row\" />\n    24\t      ))}\n    25\t    </div>\n    26\t  );\n    27\t}\n    28\t\n    29\tfunction RequireBundle({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {\n    30\t  const { state, retry } = useBundleState();\n    31\t  if (state.status === \"loading\") return <Skeleton />;\n    32\t  if (state.status === \"error\")\n    33\t    return (\n    34\t      <div className=\"container error-box\" role=\"alert\">\n    35\t        <p>\n    36\t          The benchmark data could not be loaded ({state.error}).{\" \"}\n    37\t          <button type=\"button\" className=\"linkbtn\" onClick={retry}>\n    38\t            Retry\n    39\t          </button>\n    40\t        </p>\n    41\t      </div>\n    42\t    );\n    43\t  return (\n    44\t    <>\n    45\t      {state.loaded.fallback && (\n    46\t        <div className=\"container notice\" role=\"status\">\n    47\t          Showing bundled copy of {state.loaded.file.replace(/\\.json$/, \"\")}; live data unavailable.\n    48\t        </div>\n    49\t      )}\n    50\t      {children}\n    51\t    </>\n    52\t  );\n    53\t}\n    54\t\n    55\tfunction ScrollManager() {\n    56\t  const { pathname, hash } = useLocation();\n    57\t  useEffect(() => {\n    58\t    if (hash) {\n    59\t      // The target may not exist yet: the bundle and the lazily loaded page mount after navigation.\n    60\t      // Poll briefly until it appears, then scroll once.\n    61\t      const id = decodeURIComponent(hash.slice(1));\n    62\t      let tries = 0;\n    63\t      let t = 0;\n    64\t      const seek = () => {\n    65\t        const el = document.getElementById(id);\n    66\t        if (el) el.scrollIntoView();\n    67\t        else if (tries++ < 60) t = window.setTimeout(seek, 50);\n    68\t      };\n    69\t      t = window.setTimeout(seek, 0);\n    70\t      return () => window.clearTimeout(t);\n    71\t    }\n    72\t    window.scrollTo(0, 0);\n    73\t  }, [pathname, hash]);\n    74\t  return null;\n    75\t}\n    76\t\n    77\tconst TITLES: Record<string, string> = {\n    78\t  \"/\": \"True Base Bench\",\n    79\t  \"/findings\": \"Findings · True Base Bench\",\n    80\t  \"/methodology\": \"Methodology · True Base Bench\",\n    81\t  \"/measure\": \"Measure your model · True Base Bench\",\n    82\t  \"/about\": \"About · True Base Bench\",\n    83\t};\n    84\tfunction TitleManager() {\n    85\t  const { pathname } = useLocation();\n    86\t  useEffect(() => {\n    87\t    if (TITLES[pathname]) document.title = TITLES[pathname];\n    88\t  }, [pathname]);\n    89\t  return null;\n    90\t}\n    91\t\n    92\texport function NotFound() {\n    93\t  return (\n    94\t    <div className=\"container page-head\">\n    95\t      <h1>Not found</h1>\n    96\t      <p className=\"prose\">\n    97\t        There is no page at this address. <a href=\"/\">Back to the leaderboard</a>.\n    98\t      </p>\n    99\t    </div>\n   100\t  );\n   101\t}\n   102\t\n   103\texport function App() {\n   104\t  return (\n   105\t    <MotionProvider>\n   106\t      <BundleProvider>\n   107\t        <a className=\"skip-link\" href=\"#main\">\n   108\t          Skip to main content\n   109\t        </a>\n   110\t        <SvgDefs />\n   111\t        <ScrollManager />\n   112\t        <TitleManager />\n   113\t        <TopBar />\n   114\t        <main id=\"main\" tabIndex={-1}>\n   115\t          <RequireBundle>\n   116\t            <Suspense fallback={<Skeleton />}>\n   117\t              <Routes>\n   118\t                <Route path=\"/\" element={<Leaderboard />} />\n   119\t                <Route path=\"/model/:id\" element={<ModelPage />} />\n   120\t                <Route path=\"/findings\" element={<Findings />} />\n   121\t                <Route path=\"/methodology\" element={<Methodology />} />\n   122\t                <Route path=\"/measure\" element={<Measure />} />\n   123\t                <Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n   124\t                <Route path=\"/explainers\" element={<ExplainersReview />} />\n   125\t                <Route path=\"*\" element={<NotFound />} />\n   126\t              </Routes>\n   127\t            </Suspense>\n   128\t          </RequireBundle>\n   129\t        </main>\n   130\t        <Footer />\n   131\t      </BundleProvider>\n   132\t    </MotionProvider>\n   133\t  );\n   134\t}\n     1\timport { StrictMode } from \"react\";\n     2\timport { createRoot } from \"react-dom/client\";\n     3\timport { BrowserRouter } from \"react-router-dom\";\n     4\timport \"@fontsource-variable/source-serif-4/opsz.css\";\n     5\timport \"@fontsource-variable/source-serif-4/opsz-italic.css\";\n     6\timport \"@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans-condensed/latin-400.css\";\n     7\timport \"@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans-condensed/latin-500.css\";\n     8\timport \"@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans-condensed/latin-600.css\";\n     9\timport \"@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/latin-400.css\";\n    10\timport \"@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/latin-500.css\";\n    11\timport \"./styles/tokens.css\";\n    12\timport \"./styles/base.css\";\n    13\timport \"./styles/pages.css\";\n    14\timport { App } from \"./App\";\n    15\t\n    16\tcreateRoot(document.getElementById(\"root\")!).render(\n    17\t  <StrictMode>\n    18\t    <BrowserRouter>\n    19\t      <App />\n    20\t    </BrowserRouter>\n    21\t  </StrictMode>,\n    22\t);\n     1\timport { createContext, useCallback, useContext, useEffect, useState, type ReactNode } from \"react\";\n     2\timport { loadBundle } from \"./load\";\n     3\timport type { Bundle, LoadedBundle } from \"./types\";\n     4\t\n     5\ttype State =\n     6\t  | { status: \"loading\" }\n     7\t  | { status: \"error\"; error: string }\n     8\t  | { status: \"ready\"; loaded: LoadedBundle };\n     9\t\n    10\tconst Ctx = createContext<{ state: State; retry: () => void }>({ state: { status: \"loading\" }, retry: () => {} });\n    11\t\n    12\texport function BundleProvider({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {\n    13\t  const [state, setState] = useState<State>({ status: \"loading\" });\n    14\t  const run = useCallback((force: boolean) => {\n    15\t    setState({ status: \"loading\" });\n    16\t    loadBundle(force).then(\n    17\t      (loaded) => setState({ status: \"ready\", loaded }),\n    18\t      (e: unknown) => setState({ status: \"error\", error: e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e) }),\n    19\t    );\n    20\t  }, []);\n    21\t  useEffect(() => run(false), [run]);\n    22\t  return <Ctx.Provider value={{ state, retry: () => run(true) }}>{children}</Ctx.Provider>;\n    23\t}\n    24\t\n    25\texport function useBundleState() {\n    26\t  return useContext(Ctx);\n    27\t}\n    28\t\n    29\t/** Only call under <RequireBundle>. */\n    30\texport function useBundle(): Bundle {\n    31\t  const { state } = useContext(Ctx);\n    32\t  if (state.status !== \"ready\") throw new Error(\"bundle not loaded\");\n    33\t  return state.loaded.bundle;\n    34\t}\n    35\texport function useLoaded(): LoadedBundle {\n    36\t  const { state } = useContext(Ctx);\n    37\t  if (state.status !== \"ready\") throw new Error(\"bundle not loaded\");\n    38\t  return state.loaded;\n    39\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba site/src/data/load.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/parse.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/validate.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/scoring.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/method.ts && nl -ba site/src/lib/config.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/types.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/derive.ts'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba site/src/data/load.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/parse.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/validate.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/scoring.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/method.ts && nl -ba site/src/lib/config.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/types.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/derive.ts'","aggregated_output":"     1\t/**\n     2\t * Data loading (DESIGN §6.1): manifest -> highest bench_v<N>.json -> validate.\n     3\t * Live source: VITE_DATA_BASE_URL (OVH Object Storage). Fallback: the repo copy served at /data.\n     4\t */\n     5\timport { parseBundleJson } from \"./parse\";\n     6\timport { assertBundle } from \"./validate\";\n     7\timport type { LoadedBundle, Manifest } from \"./types\";\n     8\t\n     9\tconst REPO_BASE = `${(import.meta.env?.BASE_URL ?? \"/\").replace(/\\/$/, \"\")}/data`;\n    10\t\n    11\texport function liveBase(): string | null {\n    12\t  const v = (import.meta.env?.VITE_DATA_BASE_URL as string | undefined)?.trim();\n    13\t  return v ? v.replace(/\\/+$/, \"\") : null;\n    14\t}\n    15\t\n    16\texport function bundleVersion(file: string): number {\n    17\t  const m = /bench_v(\\d+)\\.json$/.exec(file);\n    18\t  return m ? parseInt(m[1], 10) : -1;\n    19\t}\n    20\t\n    21\t/** Pick the bundle with the highest N among manifest entries named bench_v<N>.json. */\n    22\texport function latestFromManifest(man: Manifest): string {\n    23\t  const files = (man.bundles ?? []).map((b) => (typeof b === \"string\" ? b : b.file)).filter((f) => bundleVersion(f) >= 0);\n    24\t  if (!files.length) {\n    25\t    if (man.latest) return man.latest;\n    26\t    throw new Error(\"manifest lists no bench_v<N>.json bundles\");\n    27\t  }\n    28\t  return files.sort((a, b) => bundleVersion(b) - bundleVersion(a))[0];\n    29\t}\n    30\t\n    31\t/** How long the live source may take to start responding, per request, before the repo copy is used instead. */\n    32\texport const LIVE_TIMEOUT_MS = 8000;\n    33\t\n    34\tasync function fetchText(url: string, timeoutMs?: number): Promise<string> {\n    35\t  // A blackholed or very slow bucket must not leave the page on its skeleton: abort and fall back.\n    36\t  const ctl = timeoutMs && typeof AbortController !== \"undefined\" ? new AbortController() : null;\n    37\t  const timer = ctl ? setTimeout(() => ctl.abort(), timeoutMs) : null;\n    38\t  try {\n    39\t    const r = await fetch(url, { cache: \"no-cache\", signal: ctl?.signal });\n    40\t    // the limit is on getting a response at all; a slow but live connection may take longer for the body\n    41\t    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);\n    42\t    if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`${url}: HTTP ${r.status}`);\n    43\t    return await r.text();\n    44\t  } catch (e) {\n    45\t    if (ctl?.signal.aborted) throw new Error(`${url}: no response within ${timeoutMs} ms`);\n    46\t    throw e;\n    47\t  } finally {\n    48\t    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);\n    49\t  }\n    50\t}\n    51\t\n    52\tasync function loadFrom(base: string, timeoutMs?: number): Promise<Omit<LoadedBundle, \"fallback\">> {\n    53\t  const man = parseBundleJson<Manifest>(await fetchText(`${base}/manifest.json`, timeoutMs));\n    54\t  const file = latestFromManifest(man);\n    55\t  const url = `${base}/${file}`;\n    56\t  const bundle = assertBundle(parseBundleJson(await fetchText(url, timeoutMs)));\n    57\t  return { bundle, file, url };\n    58\t}\n    59\t\n    60\tlet memo: Promise<LoadedBundle> | null = null;\n    61\t\n    62\texport function loadBundle(force = false): Promise<LoadedBundle> {\n    63\t  if (memo && !force) return memo;\n    64\t  memo = (async () => {\n    65\t    const live = liveBase();\n    66\t    if (live && live !== REPO_BASE) {\n    67\t      try {\n    68\t        return { ...(await loadFrom(live, LIVE_TIMEOUT_MS)), fallback: false };\n    69\t      } catch (e) {\n    70\t        console.warn(\"[tbb] live data unavailable, using bundled copy:\", e);\n    71\t        return { ...(await loadFrom(REPO_BASE)), fallback: true };\n    72\t      }\n    73\t    }\n    74\t    return { ...(await loadFrom(REPO_BASE)), fallback: false };\n    75\t  })();\n    76\t  memo.catch(() => {\n    77\t    memo = null;\n    78\t  });\n    79\t  return memo;\n    80\t}\n     1\t/**\n     2\t * The analysis scripts write Python-style JSON, which can contain bare NaN / Infinity tokens\n     3\t * (e.g. surnames.junk for models whose sampled lists did not run). JSON.parse rejects them,\n     4\t * so they are mapped to null outside string literals before parsing. Null means \"not measured\".\n     5\t */\n     6\texport function sanitizeJsonText(text: string): string {\n     7\t  let out = \"\";\n     8\t  let i = 0;\n     9\t  let last = 0;\n    10\t  const n = text.length;\n    11\t  while (i < n) {\n    12\t    const c = text[i];\n    13\t    if (c === '\"') {\n    14\t      i++;\n    15\t      while (i < n && text[i] !== '\"') i += text[i] === \"\\\\\" ? 2 : 1;\n    16\t      i++;\n    17\t      continue;\n    18\t    }\n    19\t    let tok: string | null = null;\n    20\t    if (c === \"N\" && text.startsWith(\"NaN\", i)) tok = \"NaN\";\n    21\t    else if (c === \"I\" && text.startsWith(\"Infinity\", i)) tok = \"Infinity\";\n    22\t    else if (c === \"-\" && text.startsWith(\"-Infinity\", i)) tok = \"-Infinity\";\n    23\t    if (tok) {\n    24\t      out += text.slice(last, i) + \"null\";\n    25\t      i += tok.length;\n    26\t      last = i;\n    27\t      continue;\n    28\t    }\n    29\t    i++;\n    30\t  }\n    31\t  return last === 0 ? text : out + text.slice(last);\n    32\t}\n    33\t\n    34\texport function parseBundleJson<T = unknown>(text: string): T {\n    35\t  return JSON.parse(sanitizeJsonText(text)) as T;\n    36\t}\n     1\t/**\n     2\t * Runtime structural validation of a bundle (DESIGN §6.1 step 2). The full JSON Schema lives in\n     3\t * bench.schema.json and is enforced in tests; this is the cheap check run in the browser.\n     4\t */\n     5\timport { STAGES, SUB_KEYS, type Bundle } from \"./types\";\n     6\t\n     7\texport const SUPPORTED_SCHEMA_VERSIONS = [1];\n     8\t\n     9\texport function validateBundle(b: unknown): string[] {\n    10\t  const errs: string[] = [];\n    11\t  if (!b || typeof b !== \"object\") return [\"bundle is not an object\"];\n    12\t  const o = b as Record<string, unknown>;\n    13\t  for (const k of [\"schema_version\", \"generated\", \"n_models\", \"reference\", \"models\"]) {\n    14\t    if (!(k in o)) errs.push(`missing top-level key ${k}`);\n    15\t  }\n    16\t  if (typeof o.schema_version !== \"number\" || !SUPPORTED_SCHEMA_VERSIONS.includes(o.schema_version))\n    17\t    errs.push(`unsupported schema_version ${String(o.schema_version)}`);\n    18\t  if (!Array.isArray(o.models)) {\n    19\t    errs.push(\"models is not an array\");\n    20\t    return errs;\n    21\t  }\n    22\t  if (typeof o.n_models === \"number\" && o.n_models !== o.models.length)\n    23\t    errs.push(`n_models ${o.n_models} != models.length ${o.models.length}`);\n    24\t  const ids = new Set<string>();\n    25\t  (o.models as unknown[]).forEach((m, i) => {\n    26\t    if (!m || typeof m !== \"object\") {\n    27\t      errs.push(`models[${i}] is not an object`);\n    28\t      return;\n    29\t    }\n    30\t    const r = m as Record<string, unknown>;\n    31\t    const where = `models[${typeof r.id === \"string\" ? r.id : i}]`;\n    32\t    for (const k of [\"id\", \"family\", \"label\"]) if (typeof r[k] !== \"string\") errs.push(`${where}.${k} must be a string`);\n    33\t    if (typeof r.id === \"string\") {\n    34\t      if (ids.has(r.id)) errs.push(`${where} duplicate id`);\n    35\t      ids.add(r.id);\n    36\t    }\n    37\t    if (!STAGES.includes(r.stage as never)) errs.push(`${where}.stage invalid: ${String(r.stage)}`);\n    38\t    if (typeof r.order !== \"number\") errs.push(`${where}.order must be a number`);\n    39\t    if (r.score !== null && typeof r.score !== \"number\") errs.push(`${where}.score must be a number or null`);\n    40\t    if (!r.subscores || typeof r.subscores !== \"object\") errs.push(`${where}.subscores missing`);\n    41\t    else for (const k of SUB_KEYS) if (!(k in (r.subscores as object))) errs.push(`${where}.subscores.${k} missing`);\n    42\t  });\n    43\t  return errs;\n    44\t}\n    45\t\n    46\texport function assertBundle(b: unknown): Bundle {\n    47\t  const errs = validateBundle(b);\n    48\t  if (errs.length) throw new Error(\"Invalid bundle: \" + errs.slice(0, 5).join(\"; \"));\n    49\t  return b as Bundle;\n    50\t}\n     1\t/**\n     2\t * Recompute sub-scores and the overall score from a fingerprint, exactly as docs/vh_truebase.py does.\n     3\t * sub = clip((x - bad) / (good - bad), 0, 1); calib uses min(gap, 0). Overall = mean of available sub-scores.\n     4\t */\n     5\timport { RAMPS, SOURCES } from \"./method\";\n     6\timport { SUB_KEYS, type SubKey } from \"./types\";\n     7\t\n     8\texport type Ramps = Record<SubKey, [number, number]>;\n     9\t\n    10\tconst isNum = (x: unknown): x is number => typeof x === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(x);\n    11\t\n    12\texport function ramp(x: number | null | undefined, good: number, bad: number): number | null {\n    13\t  if (!isNum(x)) return null;\n    14\t  const t = (x - bad) / (good - bad);\n    15\t  return Math.max(0, Math.min(1, t));\n    16\t}\n    17\t\n    18\texport function pick(f: Record<string, unknown> | null | undefined, keys: string[]): { value: number | null; source: string | null } {\n    19\t  if (!f) return { value: null, source: null };\n    20\t  for (const k of keys) {\n    21\t    const v = f[k];\n    22\t    if (isNum(v)) return { value: v, source: k };\n    23\t  }\n    24\t  return { value: null, source: null };\n    25\t}\n    26\t\n    27\texport interface Recomputed {\n    28\t  subscores: Record<SubKey, { score: number | null; value: number | null; source: string | null }>;\n    29\t  overall: number | null;\n    30\t  nAvail: number;\n    31\t}\n    32\t\n    33\texport function subScore(key: SubKey, value: number | null, ramps: Ramps = RAMPS): number | null {\n    34\t  if (value === null) return null;\n    35\t  const [good, bad] = ramps[key];\n    36\t  return ramp(key === \"calib\" ? Math.min(value, 0) : value, good, bad);\n    37\t}\n    38\t\n    39\texport function recompute(fingerprint: Record<string, unknown> | null | undefined, ramps: Ramps = RAMPS): Recomputed {\n    40\t  const subscores = {} as Recomputed[\"subscores\"];\n    41\t  for (const k of SUB_KEYS) {\n    42\t    const { value, source } = pick(fingerprint, SOURCES[k]);\n    43\t    subscores[k] = { score: subScore(k, value, ramps), value, source };\n    44\t  }\n    45\t  const avail = SUB_KEYS.map((k) => subscores[k].score).filter(isNum);\n    46\t  let sum = 0;\n    47\t  for (const a of avail) sum += a;\n    48\t  return { subscores, overall: avail.length ? sum / avail.length : null, nAvail: avail.length };\n    49\t}\n    50\t\n    51\t/** Mean of the sub-scores as stored in the bundle (what the arithmetic line shows). */\n    52\texport function meanOfStored(subs: Record<SubKey, { score: number | null } | null>): { mean: number | null; parts: (number | null)[]; n: number } {\n    53\t  const parts = SUB_KEYS.map((k) => (subs[k] && isNum(subs[k]!.score) ? (subs[k]!.score as number) : null));\n    54\t  const avail = parts.filter(isNum);\n    55\t  let sum = 0;\n    56\t  for (const a of avail) sum += a;\n    57\t  return { mean: avail.length ? sum / avail.length : null, parts, n: avail.length };\n    58\t}\n     1\t/**\n     2\t * Method constants: definitions, not measurements.\n     3\t * Ramps mirror RAMPS in docs/vh_truebase.py; surname frames mirror SURNAME_PROMPTS in docs/vh_score.py.\n     4\t * Tests (src/test/method.test.ts) keep both in sync with the scripts and with bundle.reference.* when present.\n     5\t */\n     6\timport type { SubKey } from \"./types\";\n     7\t\n     8\t/** feature -> [value that scores 1, value that scores 0] */\n     9\texport const RAMPS: Record<SubKey, [number, number]> = {\n    10\t  calib: [0.0, -0.1],\n    11\t  spread: [0.015, 0.1],\n    12\t  memo: [0.02, 0.12],\n    13\t  prior: [0.0, 0.4],\n    14\t};\n    15\t\n    16\t/** Feature lookup order per sub-score: first non-null wins. */\n    17\texport const SOURCES: Record<SubKey, string[]> = {\n    18\t  calib: [\"F_calib_gap\", \"calib_gap\"],\n    19\t  spread: [\"F_gap_domain_absmean\", \"calib_gap_domain_absmean\"],\n    20\t  memo: [\"X_runfrac_wiki\", \"verbatim_run_frac_wiki\"],\n    21\t  prior: [\"roll_instr_marker_rate\"],\n    22\t};\n    23\t\n    24\texport const SUB_META: Record<\n    25\t  SubKey,\n    26\t  { name: string; short: string; feature: string; unit: \"nats\" | \"share\"; readsAs: string; clipPositive?: boolean }\n    27\t> = {\n    28\t  calib: {\n    29\t    name: \"Calib\",\n    30\t    short: \"C\",\n    31\t    feature: \"F_calib_gap\",\n    32\t    unit: \"nats\",\n    33\t    readsAs: \"over-confidence; pre-anneal checkpoints sit near 0\",\n    34\t    clipPositive: true,\n    35\t  },\n    36\t  spread: {\n    37\t    name: \"Spread\",\n    38\t    short: \"S\",\n    39\t    feature: \"F_gap_domain_absmean\",\n    40\t    unit: \"nats\",\n    41\t    readsAs: \"miscalibration spreading across genres\",\n    42\t  },\n    43\t  memo: {\n    44\t    name: \"Memo\",\n    45\t    short: \"M\",\n    46\t    feature: \"X_runfrac_wiki\",\n    47\t    unit: \"share\",\n    48\t    readsAs: \"recitation of the anneal mix\",\n    49\t  },\n    50\t  prior: {\n    51\t    name: \"Prior\",\n    52\t    short: \"P\",\n    53\t    feature: \"roll_instr_marker_rate\",\n    54\t    unit: \"share\",\n    55\t    readsAs: \"instruction-shaped prior\",\n    56\t  },\n    57\t};\n    58\t\n    59\t/** Verbatim-run rule (docs/vh_memo.py): runs of >= RUN_MIN_LEN tokens with top-1 = real and H < RUN_H_MAX. */\n    60\texport const RUN_MIN_LEN = 16;\n    61\texport const RUN_H_MAX = 0.2;\n    62\t\n    63\t/** Entropy threshold of the own-sample collapse feature roll_frac_H<0.1 (docs/vh_analyze.py). */\n    64\texport const ROLL_H_LOW = 0.1;\n    65\t\n    66\t/** Number of US Census 2010 surnames used as the in-Census reference for junk share. */\n    67\texport const CENSUS_SURNAMES = 162253;\n    68\t\n    69\texport const SURNAME_PROMPTS: Record<string, string> = {\n    70\t  pop: \"Below is a list of 100 American surnames drawn at random from the U.S. population, so common surnames appear more often than rare ones. The list is in random order, not alphabetical.\\n\\n1.\",\n    71\t  census:\n    72\t    \"Random sample of 100 U.S. last names, drawn in proportion to their frequency in the 2010 Census, listed in the order they were drawn:\\n\\n1.\",\n    73\t  plain: \"Here are the last names of 100 randomly chosen Americans, in no particular order:\\n\\n1.\",\n    74\t  fewshot1:\n    75\t    \"Last names of six randomly selected U.S. residents:\\n\\n1. Collins\\n2. Johnson\\n3. Whitaker\\n4. Matias\\n5. Trujillo\\n6. Oneill\\n7.\",\n    76\t  fewshot2:\n    77\t    \"Last names of six randomly selected U.S. residents:\\n\\n1. Watson\\n2. Rubio\\n3. Adkins\\n4. Beltz\\n5. Evans\\n6. Carlson\\n7.\",\n    78\t  fewshot3:\n    79\t    \"Last names of six randomly selected U.S. residents:\\n\\n1. Graham\\n2. Brock\\n3. Calderon\\n4. Medina\\n5. Danforth\\n6. Morris\\n7.\",\n    80\t};\n    81\t\n    82\texport const SURNAME_FRAMES = {\n    83\t  desc: [\"pop\", \"census\", \"plain\"],\n    84\t  few: [\"fewshot1\", \"fewshot2\", \"fewshot3\"],\n    85\t};\n    86\t\n    87\t/** Named method constants addressable from markdown as {{def:NAME|fmt}}. */\n    88\texport const METHOD_DEFS: Record<string, number> = {\n    89\t  RUN_MIN_LEN,\n    90\t  RUN_H_MAX,\n    91\t  CENSUS_SURNAMES,\n    92\t  \"calib.one\": RAMPS.calib[0],\n    93\t  \"calib.zero\": RAMPS.calib[1],\n    94\t  \"spread.one\": RAMPS.spread[0],\n    95\t  \"spread.zero\": RAMPS.spread[1],\n    96\t  \"memo.one\": RAMPS.memo[0],\n    97\t  \"memo.zero\": RAMPS.memo[1],\n    98\t  \"prior.one\": RAMPS.prior[0],\n    99\t  \"prior.zero\": RAMPS.prior[1],\n   100\t};\n     1\t/** Site-level configuration from the build environment. */\n     2\texport const ORG_URL = \"https://github.com/anima-research\";\n     3\t/** Optional: repository URL used to link script names (e.g. https://github.com/<org>/<repo>). */\n     4\texport const SOURCE_REPO_URL = ((import.meta.env?.VITE_SOURCE_REPO_URL as string | undefined) ?? \"\").replace(/\\/+$/, \"\");\n     5\texport function docsLink(file: string): string | null {\n     6\t  return SOURCE_REPO_URL ? `${SOURCE_REPO_URL}/blob/main/docs/${file}` : null;\n     7\t}\n     1\t/** Types for the bench bundle (docs/SPEC.md, \"Data contract\"). Every block may be null. */\n     2\t\n     3\texport type Stage = \"pre-anneal\" | \"anneal\" | \"base\" | \"post-trained\";\n     4\texport const STAGES: Stage[] = [\"pre-anneal\", \"anneal\", \"base\", \"post-trained\"];\n     5\t\n     6\texport type SubKey = \"calib\" | \"spread\" | \"memo\" | \"prior\";\n     7\texport const SUB_KEYS: SubKey[] = [\"calib\", \"spread\", \"memo\", \"prior\"];\n     8\t\n     9\texport interface SubScore {\n    10\t  score: number | null;\n    11\t  value: number | null;\n    12\t  source: string | null;\n    13\t}\n    14\t\n    15\t/** [lo, hi, count, mean_confidence, accuracy] */\n    16\texport type ReliabilityBin = [number, number, number, number | null, number | null];\n    17\t\n    18\texport interface Reliability {\n    19\t  ECE?: number | null;\n    20\t  top1_acc?: number | null;\n    21\t  mean_p1?: number | null;\n    22\t  \"frac_p1>0.9\"?: number | null;\n    23\t  \"acc_minus_conf_p1>0.9\"?: number | null;\n    24\t  bins?: ReliabilityBin[] | null;\n    25\t  [k: string]: unknown;\n    26\t}\n    27\t\n    28\texport interface Lexical {\n    29\t  ttr?: number | null;\n    30\t  hapax?: number | null;\n    31\t  distinct4?: number | null;\n    32\t  zipf_slope?: number | null;\n    33\t  gzip_ratio?: number | null;\n    34\t  [k: string]: unknown;\n    35\t}\n    36\t\n    37\texport interface TailPass {\n    38\t  \"s>7\"?: number | null;\n    39\t  \"s>10\"?: number | null;\n    40\t  \"rank>100\"?: number | null;\n    41\t  \"rank>1000\"?: number | null;\n    42\t  CE?: number | null;\n    43\t  H?: number | null;\n    44\t  s_p99?: number | null;\n    45\t  gap_hiH?: number | null;\n    46\t  [k: string]: number | null | undefined;\n    47\t}\n    48\t\n    49\texport type TailKey = \"tf\" | \"tf_fresh\" | \"tf_weird\";\n    50\t\n    51\texport interface TopName {\n    52\t  name: string;\n    53\t  p_census?: number | null;\n    54\t  p_desc?: number | null;\n    55\t  p_few?: number | null;\n    56\t}\n    57\t\n    58\texport interface Surnames {\n    59\t  KL_desc?: number | null;\n    60\t  KL_few?: number | null;\n    61\t  H_desc?: number | null;\n    62\t  H_few?: number | null;\n    63\t  H_r?: number | null;\n    64\t  top10_desc?: number | null;\n    65\t  top10_few?: number | null;\n    66\t  top10_r?: number | null;\n    67\t  smith_plain?: number | null;\n    68\t  junk?: number | null;\n    69\t  distinct?: number | null;\n    70\t  surpr?: number | null;\n    71\t  top_names?: TopName[] | null;\n    72\t  /** name -> log P(name | frame); flat, or keyed by frame. Optional. */\n    73\t  logp_top50?: Record<string, number> | Record<string, Record<string, number>> | null;\n    74\t  [k: string]: unknown;\n    75\t}\n    76\t\n    77\t/** genre -> [gap, verbatim share, CE] */\n    78\texport type Weird = Record<string, [number | null, number | null, number | null] | null>;\n    79\t\n    80\texport interface Model {\n    81\t  id: string;\n    82\t  family: string;\n    83\t  stage: Stage;\n    84\t  label: string;\n    85\t  order: number;\n    86\t  score: number | null;\n    87\t  subscores: Record<SubKey, SubScore | null>;\n    88\t  fingerprint: Record<string, number | null> | null;\n    89\t  surnames: Surnames | null;\n    90\t  weird: Weird | null;\n    91\t  selfboot: { reliability?: Reliability | null; lexical?: Lexical | null } | null;\n    92\t  tail: Partial<Record<TailKey, TailPass | null>> | null;\n    93\t  params?: { total_b?: number | null; active_b?: number | null } | null;\n    94\t  [k: string]: unknown;\n    95\t}\n    96\t\n    97\texport interface Reference {\n    98\t  census_entropy_top2000?: number | null;\n    99\t  census_self_entropy_all?: number | null;\n   100\t  pile_lexical?: Lexical | null;\n   101\t  ramps?: Record<string, [number, number]> | null;\n   102\t  method?: { run_min_len?: number; run_H_max?: number; marker_list?: string[] } | null;\n   103\t  surname_frames?: { desc?: string[]; few?: string[] } | null;\n   104\t  passes?: Record<string, unknown> | null;\n   105\t  [k: string]: unknown;\n   106\t}\n   107\t\n   108\texport interface Bundle {\n   109\t  schema_version: number;\n   110\t  generated: string;\n   111\t  n_models: number;\n   112\t  reference: Reference;\n   113\t  models: Model[];\n   114\t  explainers?: Record<string, unknown> | null;\n   115\t  [k: string]: unknown;\n   116\t}\n   117\t\n   118\texport interface ManifestEntry {\n   119\t  file: string;\n   120\t  schema_version?: number;\n   121\t  generated?: string;\n   122\t  n_models?: number;\n   123\t}\n   124\t\n   125\texport interface Manifest {\n   126\t  manifest_version?: number;\n   127\t  latest?: string;\n   128\t  bundles: (ManifestEntry | string)[];\n   129\t}\n   130\t\n   131\t/** A loaded bundle plus where it came from. */\n   132\texport interface LoadedBundle {\n   133\t  bundle: Bundle;\n   134\t  file: string;\n   135\t  url: string;\n   136\t  /** true when the live source failed and the repo copy is shown */\n   137\t  fallback: boolean;\n   138\t}\n     1\t/** Derived helpers (DESIGN §6.3, §6.4). Everything is computed from the bundle; no ids are hardcoded. */\n     2\timport { fp, get } from \"./bundle\";\n     3\timport { RAMPS } from \"./method\";\n     4\timport { STAGES, SUB_KEYS, type Bundle, type Model, type Stage, type SubKey } from \"./types\";\n     5\t\n     6\texport const stageOrder: Stage[] = STAGES;\n     7\texport const STAGE_CODE: Record<Stage, string> = { \"pre-anneal\": \"PRE\", anneal: \"ANL\", base: \"BASE\", \"post-trained\": \"POST\" };\n     8\texport const stageIndex = (s: Stage) => stageOrder.indexOf(s);\n     9\t\n    10\t/** Rungs of a family sorted by order. */\n    11\texport function ladder(bundle: Bundle, family: string): Model[] {\n    12\t  return bundle.models.filter((m) => m.family === family).sort((a, b) => a.order - b.order);\n    13\t}\n    14\t\n    15\t/** Group a ladder into columns: consecutive rungs with the same stage are parallel releases (DESIGN §6.3). */\n    16\texport function ladderColumns(rungs: Model[]): Model[][] {\n    17\t  const cols: Model[][] = [];\n    18\t  for (const r of rungs) {\n    19\t    const last = cols[cols.length - 1];\n    20\t    if (last && last[0].stage === r.stage && r.stage !== \"anneal\" && r.stage !== \"pre-anneal\" && r.stage !== \"post-trained\") last.push(r);\n    21\t    else cols.push([r]);\n    22\t  }\n    23\t  return cols;\n    24\t}\n    25\t\n    26\texport function familiesOf(bundle: Bundle): string[] {\n    27\t  return Array.from(new Set(bundle.models.map((m) => m.family))).sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b));\n    28\t}\n    29\t\n    30\texport interface Params {\n    31\t  total_b: number | null;\n    32\t  active_b: number | null;\n    33\t}\n    34\t\n    35\t/** Parameter count from models[].params, else the family string, else a B-only match on the label. */\n    36\texport function params(m: Pick<Model, \"family\" | \"label\" | \"params\">): Params {\n    37\t  if (m.params && typeof m.params.total_b === \"number\")\n    38\t    return { total_b: m.params.total_b, active_b: typeof m.params.active_b === \"number\" ? m.params.active_b : null };\n    39\t  const re = /(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?)([BT])(?:-A(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?)B)?/;\n    40\t  const f = re.exec(m.family);\n    41\t  if (f) {\n    42\t    const total = parseFloat(f[1]) * (f[2] === \"T\" ? 1000 : 1);\n    43\t    return { total_b: total, active_b: f[3] ? parseFloat(f[3]) : null };\n    44\t  }\n    45\t  const l = re.exec(m.label);\n    46\t  if (l && l[2] === \"B\") return { total_b: parseFloat(l[1]), active_b: l[3] ? parseFloat(l[3]) : null };\n    47\t  return { total_b: null, active_b: null };\n    48\t}\n    49\t\n    50\texport function fmtParams(p: Params): string {\n    51\t  if (p.total_b === null) return \"—\";\n    52\t  const t = (x: number) => (x >= 1000 ? `${+(x / 1000).toFixed(2)}T` : `${+x.toFixed(1)}B`);\n    53\t  return p.active_b !== null ? `${t(p.total_b)}·A${t(p.active_b)}` : t(p.total_b);\n    54\t}\n    55\t\n    56\texport type SizeBucket = \"lt15\" | \"15to100\" | \"gt100\" | \"unknown\";\n    57\texport const SIZE_LABEL: Record<SizeBucket, string> = { lt15: \"<15B\", \"15to100\": \"15–100B\", gt100: \">100B\", unknown: \"unknown\" };\n    58\texport function sizeBucket(m: Model): SizeBucket {\n    59\t  const t = params(m).total_b;\n    60\t  if (t === null) return \"unknown\";\n    61\t  return t < 15 ? \"lt15\" : t <= 100 ? \"15to100\" : \"gt100\";\n    62\t}\n    63\t\n    64\tconst DOMAIN_NAMES: Record<string, string> = {\n    65\t  ao3_englis: \"AO3 (English)\",\n    66\t  ao3_noneng: \"AO3 (non-English)\",\n    67\t  arxiv_cs: \"arXiv (CS)\",\n    68\t  arxiv_math: \"arXiv (math)\",\n    69\t  arxiv_othe: \"arXiv (other)\",\n    70\t  arxiv_phys: \"arXiv (physics)\",\n    71\t  bbc_news: \"BBC News\",\n    72\t  biorxiv_al: \"bioRxiv\",\n    73\t  github_cpp: \"GitHub (C++)\",\n    74\t  github_jav: \"GitHub (Java)\",\n    75\t  github_mar: \"GitHub (Markdown)\",\n    76\t  github_oth: \"GitHub (other)\",\n    77\t  github_pyt: \"GitHub (Python)\",\n    78\t  wikipedia_: \"Wikipedia\",\n    79\t};\n    80\t/** Display name for a truncated domain key (F_CE_ao3_englis -> \"AO3 (English)\"); null if unknown. */\n    81\texport function domainName(key: string): string | null {\n    82\t  const k = key.replace(/^F_(CE|gap)_/, \"\");\n    83\t  return DOMAIN_NAMES[k] ?? null;\n    84\t}\n    85\texport function domainKeys(m: Model, prefix = \"F_CE_\"): string[] {\n    86\t  return Object.keys(m.fingerprint ?? {}).filter((k) => k.startsWith(prefix) && k !== prefix.replace(/_$/, \"\"));\n    87\t}\n    88\t\n    89\t/** Nearest preceding rung in the same family whose stage is not post-trained. */\n    90\texport function basePairFor(bundle: Bundle, post: Model): Model | null {\n    91\t  const rungs = ladder(bundle, post.family).filter((r) => r.order < post.order && r.stage !== \"post-trained\");\n    92\t  return rungs.length ? rungs[rungs.length - 1] : null;\n    93\t}\n    94\t\n    95\t/** Default compare sibling (DESIGN §7.2): nearest preceding pre/base rung for post-trained, else next rung. */\n    96\texport function defaultCompare(bundle: Bundle, m: Model): Model | null {\n    97\t  const rungs = ladder(bundle, m.family);\n    98\t  if (m.stage === \"post-trained\") {\n    99\t    const prev = rungs.filter((r) => r.order < m.order && (r.stage === \"pre-anneal\" || r.stage === \"base\"));\n   100\t    if (prev.length) return prev[prev.length - 1];\n   101\t    return basePairFor(bundle, m);\n   102\t  }\n   103\t  const next = rungs.find((r) => r.order > m.order);\n   104\t  if (next) return next;\n   105\t  const prev = rungs.filter((r) => r.order < m.order);\n   106\t  return prev.length ? prev[prev.length - 1] : null;\n   107\t}\n   108\t\n   109\t/** Ranked list by overall desc, ties by family then order. Rank ties judged on the displayed 3-dp value. */\n   110\texport function ranked(bundle: Bundle): { model: Model; rank: number; tie: boolean }[] {\n   111\t  const ms = [...bundle.models].sort(compareOverall);\n   112\t  const disp = (m: Model) => (m.score === null ? null : m.score.toFixed(3));\n   113\t  const out: { model: Model; rank: number; tie: boolean }[] = [];\n   114\t  ms.forEach((m, i) => {\n   115\t    const prev = out[i - 1];\n   116\t    if (prev && disp(prev.model) === disp(m) && disp(m) !== null) {\n   117\t      out.push({ model: m, rank: prev.rank, tie: true });\n   118\t      prev.tie = true;\n   119\t    } else out.push({ model: m, rank: i + 1, tie: false });\n   120\t  });\n   121\t  return out;\n   122\t}\n   123\t\n   124\texport function compareOverall(a: Model, b: Model): number {\n   125\t  const sa = a.score ?? -Infinity;\n   126\t  const sb = b.score ?? -Infinity;\n   127\t  if (sb !== sa) return sb - sa;\n   128\t  if (a.family !== b.family) return a.family.localeCompare(b.family);\n   129\t  return a.order - b.order;\n   130\t}\n   131\t\n   132\texport function nAvailable(m: Model): number {\n   133\t  return SUB_KEYS.filter((k) => typeof m.subscores?.[k]?.score === \"number\").length;\n   134\t}\n   135\t\n   136\texport function subScoreOf(m: Model, k: SubKey): number | null {\n   137\t  return get(m, [\"subscores\", k, \"score\"]);\n   138\t}\n   139\t\n   140\t// ---------- computed specimens (§6.4) ----------\n   141\t\n   142\t/** The family with the most rungs that has both a pre-anneal and a post-trained rung. */\n   143\texport function specimenFamily(bundle: Bundle): string | null {\n   144\t  const fams = familiesOf(bundle)\n   145\t    .map((f) => ({ f, rungs: ladder(bundle, f) }))\n   146\t    .filter(({ rungs }) => rungs.some((r) => r.stage === \"pre-anneal\") && rungs.some((r) => r.stage === \"post-trained\"))\n   147\t    .sort((a, b) => b.rungs.length - a.rungs.length || a.f.localeCompare(b.f));\n   148\t  return fams.length ? fams[0].f : null;\n   149\t}\n   150\t\n   151\t/** A = lowest-order pre-anneal rung of the family; B = its highest-order post-trained rung. */\n   152\texport function specimenPair(bundle: Bundle, family = specimenFamily(bundle)): { A: Model; B: Model } | null {\n   153\t  if (!family) return null;\n   154\t  const rungs = ladder(bundle, family);\n   155\t  const A = rungs.find((r) => r.stage === \"pre-anneal\");\n   156\t  const posts = rungs.filter((r) => r.stage === \"post-trained\");\n   157\t  if (!A || !posts.length) return null;\n   158\t  return { A, B: posts[posts.length - 1] };\n   159\t}\n   160\t\n   161\t/** ± max |F_calib_gap| over pre-anneal models, with the model that sets it. */\n   162\texport function preAnnealBand(bundle: Bundle): { band: number; model: Model } | null {\n   163\t  let best: { band: number; model: Model } | null = null;\n   164\t  for (const m of bundle.models) {\n   165\t    if (m.stage !== \"pre-anneal\") continue;\n   166\t    const g = fp(m, \"F_calib_gap\");\n   167\t    if (g === null) continue;\n   168\t    if (!best || Math.abs(g) > best.band) best = { band: Math.abs(g), model: m };\n   169\t  }\n   170\t  return best;\n   171\t}\n   172\t\n   173\t/** The pre-anneal model with the highest roll_frac_H<0.1. */\n   174\texport function collapseSpecimen(bundle: Bundle): Model | null {\n   175\t  const c = bundle.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"pre-anneal\" && fp(m, \"roll_frac_H<0.1\") !== null);\n   176\t  c.sort((a, b) => fp(b, \"roll_frac_H<0.1\")! - fp(a, \"roll_frac_H<0.1\")!);\n   177\t  return c[0] ?? null;\n   178\t}\n   179\t\n   180\texport function memoValue(m: Model): number | null {\n   181\t  return fp(m, \"X_runfrac_wiki\") ?? fp(m, \"verbatim_run_frac_wiki\");\n   182\t}\n   183\t\n   184\t/**\n   185\t * Among families with a pre-anneal rung: the (last pre-anneal rung -> first following non-post rung) pair with\n   186\t * the largest rise in X_runfrac_wiki, plus the next post-trained rung.\n   187\t */\n   188\texport function memoTriple(bundle: Bundle): Model[] | null {\n   189\t  let best: { rise: number; trio: Model[] } | null = null;\n   190\t  for (const f of familiesOf(bundle)) {\n   191\t    const rungs = ladder(bundle, f);\n   192\t    const pres = rungs.filter((r) => r.stage === \"pre-anneal\");\n   193\t    if (!pres.length) continue;\n   194\t    const lastPre = pres[pres.length - 1];\n   195\t    const next = rungs.find((r) => r.order > lastPre.order && r.stage !== \"post-trained\" && r.stage !== \"pre-anneal\");\n   196\t    if (!next) continue;\n   197\t    const a = memoValue(lastPre);\n   198\t    const b = memoValue(next);\n   199\t    if (a === null || b === null) continue;\n   200\t    const post = rungs.find((r) => r.order > next.order && r.stage === \"post-trained\");\n   201\t    const trio = post ? [lastPre, next, post] : [lastPre, next];\n   202\t    if (!best || b - a > best.rise) best = { rise: b - a, trio };\n   203\t  }\n   204\t  return best?.trio ?? null;\n   205\t}\n   206\t\n   207\t/** The model with the most sub-scores strictly inside (0, 1); ties go to the overall closest to 0.5. */\n   208\texport function rampSpecimen(bundle: Bundle): Model | null {\n   209\t  const inside = (m: Model) => SUB_KEYS.filter((k) => {\n   210\t    const s = subScoreOf(m, k);\n   211\t    return s !== null && s > 0 && s < 1;\n   212\t  }).length;\n   213\t  const ms = bundle.models.filter((m) => m.score !== null);\n   214\t  ms.sort((a, b) => inside(b) - inside(a) || Math.abs(a.score! - 0.5) - Math.abs(b.score! - 0.5) || a.id.localeCompare(b.id));\n   215\t  return ms[0] ?? null;\n   216\t}\n   217\t\n   218\t/** Among pairs with |Δoverall| < 0.005, the pair with the largest L1 distance between sub-score vectors. */\n   219\texport function sameScorePair(bundle: Bundle): { a: Model; b: Model; l1: number } | null {\n   220\t  const ms = bundle.models.filter((m) => m.score !== null && nAvailable(m) === 4);\n   221\t  let best: { a: Model; b: Model; l1: number } | null = null;\n   222\t  for (let i = 0; i < ms.length; i++)\n   223\t    for (let j = i + 1; j < ms.length; j++) {\n   224\t      const a = ms[i];\n   225\t      const b = ms[j];\n   226\t      if (Math.abs(a.score! - b.score!) >= 0.005) continue;\n   227\t      const l1 = SUB_KEYS.reduce((s, k) => s + Math.abs(subScoreOf(a, k)! - subScoreOf(b, k)!), 0);\n   228\t      if (!best || l1 > best.l1) best = { a, b, l1 };\n   229\t    }\n   230\t  if (best && compareOverall(best.a, best.b) > 0) best = { a: best.b, b: best.a, l1: best.l1 };\n   231\t  return best;\n   232\t}\n   233\t\n   234\t/** Fallback when no same-score pair: A, the base with lowest memo score, the base with lowest prior score. */\n   235\texport function recipeTrio(bundle: Bundle): Model[] | null {\n   236\t  const sp = specimenPair(bundle);\n   237\t  const bases = bundle.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"base\");\n   238\t  // ties on the clipped score (several bases sit at 0) go to the raw value furthest past the 0-anchor\n   239\t  const unclipped = (m: Model, k: SubKey) => {\n   240\t    const v = get(m, [\"subscores\", k, \"value\"]);\n   241\t    const [good, bad] = RAMPS[k];\n   242\t    return v === null ? Infinity : (v - bad) / (good - bad);\n   243\t  };\n   244\t  const lowest = (k: SubKey) =>\n   245\t    bases\n   246\t      .filter((m) => subScoreOf(m, k) !== null)\n   247\t      .sort((a, b) => subScoreOf(a, k)! - subScoreOf(b, k)! || unclipped(a, k) - unclipped(b, k))[0];\n   248\t  const lm = lowest(\"memo\");\n   249\t  const lp = lowest(\"prior\");\n   250\t  if (!sp || !lm || !lp) return null;\n   251\t  return [sp.A, lm, lp];\n   252\t}\n   253\t\n   254\texport function rangeOf(xs: (number | null)[]): [number, number] | null {\n   255\t  const v = xs.filter((x): x is number => x !== null && Number.isFinite(x));\n   256\t  if (!v.length) return null;\n   257\t  return [Math.min(...v), Math.max(...v)];\n   258\t}\n   259\t\n   260\texport function median(xs: number[]): number | null {\n   261\t  if (!xs.length) return null;\n   262\t  const s = [...xs].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n   263\t  const m = s.length >> 1;\n   264\t  return s.length % 2 ? s[m] : (s[m - 1] + s[m]) / 2;\n   265\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"The bundle contains 35 checkpoints, including four pre-anneal checkpoints from three families. I found a definite overstatement in the spec’s “within ±0.02 in every domain”: one pre-anneal model’s mean absolute domain gap is already 0.02084, so at least one domain exceeds that bound.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba site/src/data/load.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/parse.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/validate.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/scoring.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/method.ts && nl -ba site/src/lib/config.ts'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba site/src/data/load.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/parse.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/validate.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/scoring.ts && nl -ba site/src/data/method.ts && nl -ba site/src/lib/config.ts'","aggregated_output":"     1\t/**\n     2\t * Data loading (DESIGN §6.1): manifest -> highest bench_v<N>.json -> validate.\n     3\t * Live source: VITE_DATA_BASE_URL (OVH Object Storage). Fallback: the repo copy served at /data.\n     4\t */\n     5\timport { parseBundleJson } from \"./parse\";\n     6\timport { assertBundle } from \"./validate\";\n     7\timport type { LoadedBundle, Manifest } from \"./types\";\n     8\t\n     9\tconst REPO_BASE = `${(import.meta.env?.BASE_URL ?? \"/\").replace(/\\/$/, \"\")}/data`;\n    10\t\n    11\texport function liveBase(): string | null {\n    12\t  const v = (import.meta.env?.VITE_DATA_BASE_URL as string | undefined)?.trim();\n    13\t  return v ? v.replace(/\\/+$/, \"\") : null;\n    14\t}\n    15\t\n    16\texport function bundleVersion(file: string): number {\n    17\t  const m = /bench_v(\\d+)\\.json$/.exec(file);\n    18\t  return m ? parseInt(m[1], 10) : -1;\n    19\t}\n    20\t\n    21\t/** Pick the bundle with the highest N among manifest entries named bench_v<N>.json. */\n    22\texport function latestFromManifest(man: Manifest): string {\n    23\t  const files = (man.bundles ?? []).map((b) => (typeof b === \"string\" ? b : b.file)).filter((f) => bundleVersion(f) >= 0);\n    24\t  if (!files.length) {\n    25\t    if (man.latest) return man.latest;\n    26\t    throw new Error(\"manifest lists no bench_v<N>.json bundles\");\n    27\t  }\n    28\t  return files.sort((a, b) => bundleVersion(b) - bundleVersion(a))[0];\n    29\t}\n    30\t\n    31\t/** How long the live source may take to start responding, per request, before the repo copy is used instead. */\n    32\texport const LIVE_TIMEOUT_MS = 8000;\n    33\t\n    34\tasync function fetchText(url: string, timeoutMs?: number): Promise<string> {\n    35\t  // A blackholed or very slow bucket must not leave the page on its skeleton: abort and fall back.\n    36\t  const ctl = timeoutMs && typeof AbortController !== \"undefined\" ? new AbortController() : null;\n    37\t  const timer = ctl ? setTimeout(() => ctl.abort(), timeoutMs) : null;\n    38\t  try {\n    39\t    const r = await fetch(url, { cache: \"no-cache\", signal: ctl?.signal });\n    40\t    // the limit is on getting a response at all; a slow but live connection may take longer for the body\n    41\t    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);\n    42\t    if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`${url}: HTTP ${r.status}`);\n    43\t    return await r.text();\n    44\t  } catch (e) {\n    45\t    if (ctl?.signal.aborted) throw new Error(`${url}: no response within ${timeoutMs} ms`);\n    46\t    throw e;\n    47\t  } finally {\n    48\t    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);\n    49\t  }\n    50\t}\n    51\t\n    52\tasync function loadFrom(base: string, timeoutMs?: number): Promise<Omit<LoadedBundle, \"fallback\">> {\n    53\t  const man = parseBundleJson<Manifest>(await fetchText(`${base}/manifest.json`, timeoutMs));\n    54\t  const file = latestFromManifest(man);\n    55\t  const url = `${base}/${file}`;\n    56\t  const bundle = assertBundle(parseBundleJson(await fetchText(url, timeoutMs)));\n    57\t  return { bundle, file, url };\n    58\t}\n    59\t\n    60\tlet memo: Promise<LoadedBundle> | null = null;\n    61\t\n    62\texport function loadBundle(force = false): Promise<LoadedBundle> {\n    63\t  if (memo && !force) return memo;\n    64\t  memo = (async () => {\n    65\t    const live = liveBase();\n    66\t    if (live && live !== REPO_BASE) {\n    67\t      try {\n    68\t        return { ...(await loadFrom(live, LIVE_TIMEOUT_MS)), fallback: false };\n    69\t      } catch (e) {\n    70\t        console.warn(\"[tbb] live data unavailable, using bundled copy:\", e);\n    71\t        return { ...(await loadFrom(REPO_BASE)), fallback: true };\n    72\t      }\n    73\t    }\n    74\t    return { ...(await loadFrom(REPO_BASE)), fallback: false };\n    75\t  })();\n    76\t  memo.catch(() => {\n    77\t    memo = null;\n    78\t  });\n    79\t  return memo;\n    80\t}\n     1\t/**\n     2\t * The analysis scripts write Python-style JSON, which can contain bare NaN / Infinity tokens\n     3\t * (e.g. surnames.junk for models whose sampled lists did not run). JSON.parse rejects them,\n     4\t * so they are mapped to null outside string literals before parsing. Null means \"not measured\".\n     5\t */\n     6\texport function sanitizeJsonText(text: string): string {\n     7\t  let out = \"\";\n     8\t  let i = 0;\n     9\t  let last = 0;\n    10\t  const n = text.length;\n    11\t  while (i < n) {\n    12\t    const c = text[i];\n    13\t    if (c === '\"') {\n    14\t      i++;\n    15\t      while (i < n && text[i] !== '\"') i += text[i] === \"\\\\\" ? 2 : 1;\n    16\t      i++;\n    17\t      continue;\n    18\t    }\n    19\t    let tok: string | null = null;\n    20\t    if (c === \"N\" && text.startsWith(\"NaN\", i)) tok = \"NaN\";\n    21\t    else if (c === \"I\" && text.startsWith(\"Infinity\", i)) tok = \"Infinity\";\n    22\t    else if (c === \"-\" && text.startsWith(\"-Infinity\", i)) tok = \"-Infinity\";\n    23\t    if (tok) {\n    24\t      out += text.slice(last, i) + \"null\";\n    25\t      i += tok.length;\n    26\t      last = i;\n    27\t      continue;\n    28\t    }\n    29\t    i++;\n    30\t  }\n    31\t  return last === 0 ? text : out + text.slice(last);\n    32\t}\n    33\t\n    34\texport function parseBundleJson<T = unknown>(text: string): T {\n    35\t  return JSON.parse(sanitizeJsonText(text)) as T;\n    36\t}\n     1\t/**\n     2\t * Runtime structural validation of a bundle (DESIGN §6.1 step 2). The full JSON Schema lives in\n     3\t * bench.schema.json and is enforced in tests; this is the cheap check run in the browser.\n     4\t */\n     5\timport { STAGES, SUB_KEYS, type Bundle } from \"./types\";\n     6\t\n     7\texport const SUPPORTED_SCHEMA_VERSIONS = [1];\n     8\t\n     9\texport function validateBundle(b: unknown): string[] {\n    10\t  const errs: string[] = [];\n    11\t  if (!b || typeof b !== \"object\") return [\"bundle is not an object\"];\n    12\t  const o = b as Record<string, unknown>;\n    13\t  for (const k of [\"schema_version\", \"generated\", \"n_models\", \"reference\", \"models\"]) {\n    14\t    if (!(k in o)) errs.push(`missing top-level key ${k}`);\n    15\t  }\n    16\t  if (typeof o.schema_version !== \"number\" || !SUPPORTED_SCHEMA_VERSIONS.includes(o.schema_version))\n    17\t    errs.push(`unsupported schema_version ${String(o.schema_version)}`);\n    18\t  if (!Array.isArray(o.models)) {\n    19\t    errs.push(\"models is not an array\");\n    20\t    return errs;\n    21\t  }\n    22\t  if (typeof o.n_models === \"number\" && o.n_models !== o.models.length)\n    23\t    errs.push(`n_models ${o.n_models} != models.length ${o.models.length}`);\n    24\t  const ids = new Set<string>();\n    25\t  (o.models as unknown[]).forEach((m, i) => {\n    26\t    if (!m || typeof m !== \"object\") {\n    27\t      errs.push(`models[${i}] is not an object`);\n    28\t      return;\n    29\t    }\n    30\t    const r = m as Record<string, unknown>;\n    31\t    const where = `models[${typeof r.id === \"string\" ? r.id : i}]`;\n    32\t    for (const k of [\"id\", \"family\", \"label\"]) if (typeof r[k] !== \"string\") errs.push(`${where}.${k} must be a string`);\n    33\t    if (typeof r.id === \"string\") {\n    34\t      if (ids.has(r.id)) errs.push(`${where} duplicate id`);\n    35\t      ids.add(r.id);\n    36\t    }\n    37\t    if (!STAGES.includes(r.stage as never)) errs.push(`${where}.stage invalid: ${String(r.stage)}`);\n    38\t    if (typeof r.order !== \"number\") errs.push(`${where}.order must be a number`);\n    39\t    if (r.score !== null && typeof r.score !== \"number\") errs.push(`${where}.score must be a number or null`);\n    40\t    if (!r.subscores || typeof r.subscores !== \"object\") errs.push(`${where}.subscores missing`);\n    41\t    else for (const k of SUB_KEYS) if (!(k in (r.subscores as object))) errs.push(`${where}.subscores.${k} missing`);\n    42\t  });\n    43\t  return errs;\n    44\t}\n    45\t\n    46\texport function assertBundle(b: unknown): Bundle {\n    47\t  const errs = validateBundle(b);\n    48\t  if (errs.length) throw new Error(\"Invalid bundle: \" + errs.slice(0, 5).join(\"; \"));\n    49\t  return b as Bundle;\n    50\t}\n     1\t/**\n     2\t * Recompute sub-scores and the overall score from a fingerprint, exactly as docs/vh_truebase.py does.\n     3\t * sub = clip((x - bad) / (good - bad), 0, 1); calib uses min(gap, 0). Overall = mean of available sub-scores.\n     4\t */\n     5\timport { RAMPS, SOURCES } from \"./method\";\n     6\timport { SUB_KEYS, type SubKey } from \"./types\";\n     7\t\n     8\texport type Ramps = Record<SubKey, [number, number]>;\n     9\t\n    10\tconst isNum = (x: unknown): x is number => typeof x === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(x);\n    11\t\n    12\texport function ramp(x: number | null | undefined, good: number, bad: number): number | null {\n    13\t  if (!isNum(x)) return null;\n    14\t  const t = (x - bad) / (good - bad);\n    15\t  return Math.max(0, Math.min(1, t));\n    16\t}\n    17\t\n    18\texport function pick(f: Record<string, unknown> | null | undefined, keys: string[]): { value: number | null; source: string | null } {\n    19\t  if (!f) return { value: null, source: null };\n    20\t  for (const k of keys) {\n    21\t    const v = f[k];\n    22\t    if (isNum(v)) return { value: v, source: k };\n    23\t  }\n    24\t  return { value: null, source: null };\n    25\t}\n    26\t\n    27\texport interface Recomputed {\n    28\t  subscores: Record<SubKey, { score: number | null; value: number | null; source: string | null }>;\n    29\t  overall: number | null;\n    30\t  nAvail: number;\n    31\t}\n    32\t\n    33\texport function subScore(key: SubKey, value: number | null, ramps: Ramps = RAMPS): number | null {\n    34\t  if (value === null) return null;\n    35\t  const [good, bad] = ramps[key];\n    36\t  return ramp(key === \"calib\" ? Math.min(value, 0) : value, good, bad);\n    37\t}\n    38\t\n    39\texport function recompute(fingerprint: Record<string, unknown> | null | undefined, ramps: Ramps = RAMPS): Recomputed {\n    40\t  const subscores = {} as Recomputed[\"subscores\"];\n    41\t  for (const k of SUB_KEYS) {\n    42\t    const { value, source } = pick(fingerprint, SOURCES[k]);\n    43\t    subscores[k] = { score: subScore(k, value, ramps), value, source };\n    44\t  }\n    45\t  const avail = SUB_KEYS.map((k) => subscores[k].score).filter(isNum);\n    46\t  let sum = 0;\n    47\t  for (const a of avail) sum += a;\n    48\t  return { subscores, overall: avail.length ? sum / avail.length : null, nAvail: avail.length };\n    49\t}\n    50\t\n    51\t/** Mean of the sub-scores as stored in the bundle (what the arithmetic line shows). */\n    52\texport function meanOfStored(subs: Record<SubKey, { score: number | null } | null>): { mean: number | null; parts: (number | null)[]; n: number } {\n    53\t  const parts = SUB_KEYS.map((k) => (subs[k] && isNum(subs[k]!.score) ? (subs[k]!.score as number) : null));\n    54\t  const avail = parts.filter(isNum);\n    55\t  let sum = 0;\n    56\t  for (const a of avail) sum += a;\n    57\t  return { mean: avail.length ? sum / avail.length : null, parts, n: avail.length };\n    58\t}\n     1\t/**\n     2\t * Method constants: definitions, not measurements.\n     3\t * Ramps mirror RAMPS in docs/vh_truebase.py; surname frames mirror SURNAME_PROMPTS in docs/vh_score.py.\n     4\t * Tests (src/test/method.test.ts) keep both in sync with the scripts and with bundle.reference.* when present.\n     5\t */\n     6\timport type { SubKey } from \"./types\";\n     7\t\n     8\t/** feature -> [value that scores 1, value that scores 0] */\n     9\texport const RAMPS: Record<SubKey, [number, number]> = {\n    10\t  calib: [0.0, -0.1],\n    11\t  spread: [0.015, 0.1],\n    12\t  memo: [0.02, 0.12],\n    13\t  prior: [0.0, 0.4],\n    14\t};\n    15\t\n    16\t/** Feature lookup order per sub-score: first non-null wins. */\n    17\texport const SOURCES: Record<SubKey, string[]> = {\n    18\t  calib: [\"F_calib_gap\", \"calib_gap\"],\n    19\t  spread: [\"F_gap_domain_absmean\", \"calib_gap_domain_absmean\"],\n    20\t  memo: [\"X_runfrac_wiki\", \"verbatim_run_frac_wiki\"],\n    21\t  prior: [\"roll_instr_marker_rate\"],\n    22\t};\n    23\t\n    24\texport const SUB_META: Record<\n    25\t  SubKey,\n    26\t  { name: string; short: string; feature: string; unit: \"nats\" | \"share\"; readsAs: string; clipPositive?: boolean }\n    27\t> = {\n    28\t  calib: {\n    29\t    name: \"Calib\",\n    30\t    short: \"C\",\n    31\t    feature: \"F_calib_gap\",\n    32\t    unit: \"nats\",\n    33\t    readsAs: \"over-confidence; pre-anneal checkpoints sit near 0\",\n    34\t    clipPositive: true,\n    35\t  },\n    36\t  spread: {\n    37\t    name: \"Spread\",\n    38\t    short: \"S\",\n    39\t    feature: \"F_gap_domain_absmean\",\n    40\t    unit: \"nats\",\n    41\t    readsAs: \"miscalibration spreading across genres\",\n    42\t  },\n    43\t  memo: {\n    44\t    name: \"Memo\",\n    45\t    short: \"M\",\n    46\t    feature: \"X_runfrac_wiki\",\n    47\t    unit: \"share\",\n    48\t    readsAs: \"recitation of the anneal mix\",\n    49\t  },\n    50\t  prior: {\n    51\t    name: \"Prior\",\n    52\t    short: \"P\",\n    53\t    feature: \"roll_instr_marker_rate\",\n    54\t    unit: \"share\",\n    55\t    readsAs: \"instruction-shaped prior\",\n    56\t  },\n    57\t};\n    58\t\n    59\t/** Verbatim-run rule (docs/vh_memo.py): runs of >= RUN_MIN_LEN tokens with top-1 = real and H < RUN_H_MAX. */\n    60\texport const RUN_MIN_LEN = 16;\n    61\texport const RUN_H_MAX = 0.2;\n    62\t\n    63\t/** Entropy threshold of the own-sample collapse feature roll_frac_H<0.1 (docs/vh_analyze.py). */\n    64\texport const ROLL_H_LOW = 0.1;\n    65\t\n    66\t/** Number of US Census 2010 surnames used as the in-Census reference for junk share. */\n    67\texport const CENSUS_SURNAMES = 162253;\n    68\t\n    69\texport const SURNAME_PROMPTS: Record<string, string> = {\n    70\t  pop: \"Below is a list of 100 American surnames drawn at random from the U.S. population, so common surnames appear more often than rare ones. The list is in random order, not alphabetical.\\n\\n1.\",\n    71\t  census:\n    72\t    \"Random sample of 100 U.S. last names, drawn in proportion to their frequency in the 2010 Census, listed in the order they were drawn:\\n\\n1.\",\n    73\t  plain: \"Here are the last names of 100 randomly chosen Americans, in no particular order:\\n\\n1.\",\n    74\t  fewshot1:\n    75\t    \"Last names of six randomly selected U.S. residents:\\n\\n1. Collins\\n2. Johnson\\n3. Whitaker\\n4. Matias\\n5. Trujillo\\n6. Oneill\\n7.\",\n    76\t  fewshot2:\n    77\t    \"Last names of six randomly selected U.S. residents:\\n\\n1. Watson\\n2. Rubio\\n3. Adkins\\n4. Beltz\\n5. Evans\\n6. Carlson\\n7.\",\n    78\t  fewshot3:\n    79\t    \"Last names of six randomly selected U.S. residents:\\n\\n1. Graham\\n2. Brock\\n3. Calderon\\n4. Medina\\n5. Danforth\\n6. Morris\\n7.\",\n    80\t};\n    81\t\n    82\texport const SURNAME_FRAMES = {\n    83\t  desc: [\"pop\", \"census\", \"plain\"],\n    84\t  few: [\"fewshot1\", \"fewshot2\", \"fewshot3\"],\n    85\t};\n    86\t\n    87\t/** Named method constants addressable from markdown as {{def:NAME|fmt}}. */\n    88\texport const METHOD_DEFS: Record<string, number> = {\n    89\t  RUN_MIN_LEN,\n    90\t  RUN_H_MAX,\n    91\t  CENSUS_SURNAMES,\n    92\t  \"calib.one\": RAMPS.calib[0],\n    93\t  \"calib.zero\": RAMPS.calib[1],\n    94\t  \"spread.one\": RAMPS.spread[0],\n    95\t  \"spread.zero\": RAMPS.spread[1],\n    96\t  \"memo.one\": RAMPS.memo[0],\n    97\t  \"memo.zero\": RAMPS.memo[1],\n    98\t  \"prior.one\": RAMPS.prior[0],\n    99\t  \"prior.zero\": RAMPS.prior[1],\n   100\t};\n     1\t/** Site-level configuration from the build environment. */\n     2\texport const ORG_URL = \"https://github.com/anima-research\";\n     3\t/** Optional: repository URL used to link script names (e.g. https://github.com/<org>/<repo>). */\n     4\texport const SOURCE_REPO_URL = ((import.meta.env?.VITE_SOURCE_REPO_URL as string | undefined) ?? \"\").replace(/\\/+$/, \"\");\n     5\texport function docsLink(file: string): string | null {\n     6\t  return SOURCE_REPO_URL ? `${SOURCE_REPO_URL}/blob/main/docs/${file}` : null;\n     7\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba site/src/data/derive.ts && nl -ba site/src/findings.ts | sed -n '1,112p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba site/src/data/derive.ts && nl -ba site/src/findings.ts | sed -n '1,112p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t/** Derived helpers (DESIGN §6.3, §6.4). Everything is computed from the bundle; no ids are hardcoded. */\n     2\timport { fp, get } from \"./bundle\";\n     3\timport { RAMPS } from \"./method\";\n     4\timport { STAGES, SUB_KEYS, type Bundle, type Model, type Stage, type SubKey } from \"./types\";\n     5\t\n     6\texport const stageOrder: Stage[] = STAGES;\n     7\texport const STAGE_CODE: Record<Stage, string> = { \"pre-anneal\": \"PRE\", anneal: \"ANL\", base: \"BASE\", \"post-trained\": \"POST\" };\n     8\texport const stageIndex = (s: Stage) => stageOrder.indexOf(s);\n     9\t\n    10\t/** Rungs of a family sorted by order. */\n    11\texport function ladder(bundle: Bundle, family: string): Model[] {\n    12\t  return bundle.models.filter((m) => m.family === family).sort((a, b) => a.order - b.order);\n    13\t}\n    14\t\n    15\t/** Group a ladder into columns: consecutive rungs with the same stage are parallel releases (DESIGN §6.3). */\n    16\texport function ladderColumns(rungs: Model[]): Model[][] {\n    17\t  const cols: Model[][] = [];\n    18\t  for (const r of rungs) {\n    19\t    const last = cols[cols.length - 1];\n    20\t    if (last && last[0].stage === r.stage && r.stage !== \"anneal\" && r.stage !== \"pre-anneal\" && r.stage !== \"post-trained\") last.push(r);\n    21\t    else cols.push([r]);\n    22\t  }\n    23\t  return cols;\n    24\t}\n    25\t\n    26\texport function familiesOf(bundle: Bundle): string[] {\n    27\t  return Array.from(new Set(bundle.models.map((m) => m.family))).sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b));\n    28\t}\n    29\t\n    30\texport interface Params {\n    31\t  total_b: number | null;\n    32\t  active_b: number | null;\n    33\t}\n    34\t\n    35\t/** Parameter count from models[].params, else the family string, else a B-only match on the label. */\n    36\texport function params(m: Pick<Model, \"family\" | \"label\" | \"params\">): Params {\n    37\t  if (m.params && typeof m.params.total_b === \"number\")\n    38\t    return { total_b: m.params.total_b, active_b: typeof m.params.active_b === \"number\" ? m.params.active_b : null };\n    39\t  const re = /(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?)([BT])(?:-A(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?)B)?/;\n    40\t  const f = re.exec(m.family);\n    41\t  if (f) {\n    42\t    const total = parseFloat(f[1]) * (f[2] === \"T\" ? 1000 : 1);\n    43\t    return { total_b: total, active_b: f[3] ? parseFloat(f[3]) : null };\n    44\t  }\n    45\t  const l = re.exec(m.label);\n    46\t  if (l && l[2] === \"B\") return { total_b: parseFloat(l[1]), active_b: l[3] ? parseFloat(l[3]) : null };\n    47\t  return { total_b: null, active_b: null };\n    48\t}\n    49\t\n    50\texport function fmtParams(p: Params): string {\n    51\t  if (p.total_b === null) return \"—\";\n    52\t  const t = (x: number) => (x >= 1000 ? `${+(x / 1000).toFixed(2)}T` : `${+x.toFixed(1)}B`);\n    53\t  return p.active_b !== null ? `${t(p.total_b)}·A${t(p.active_b)}` : t(p.total_b);\n    54\t}\n    55\t\n    56\texport type SizeBucket = \"lt15\" | \"15to100\" | \"gt100\" | \"unknown\";\n    57\texport const SIZE_LABEL: Record<SizeBucket, string> = { lt15: \"<15B\", \"15to100\": \"15–100B\", gt100: \">100B\", unknown: \"unknown\" };\n    58\texport function sizeBucket(m: Model): SizeBucket {\n    59\t  const t = params(m).total_b;\n    60\t  if (t === null) return \"unknown\";\n    61\t  return t < 15 ? \"lt15\" : t <= 100 ? \"15to100\" : \"gt100\";\n    62\t}\n    63\t\n    64\tconst DOMAIN_NAMES: Record<string, string> = {\n    65\t  ao3_englis: \"AO3 (English)\",\n    66\t  ao3_noneng: \"AO3 (non-English)\",\n    67\t  arxiv_cs: \"arXiv (CS)\",\n    68\t  arxiv_math: \"arXiv (math)\",\n    69\t  arxiv_othe: \"arXiv (other)\",\n    70\t  arxiv_phys: \"arXiv (physics)\",\n    71\t  bbc_news: \"BBC News\",\n    72\t  biorxiv_al: \"bioRxiv\",\n    73\t  github_cpp: \"GitHub (C++)\",\n    74\t  github_jav: \"GitHub (Java)\",\n    75\t  github_mar: \"GitHub (Markdown)\",\n    76\t  github_oth: \"GitHub (other)\",\n    77\t  github_pyt: \"GitHub (Python)\",\n    78\t  wikipedia_: \"Wikipedia\",\n    79\t};\n    80\t/** Display name for a truncated domain key (F_CE_ao3_englis -> \"AO3 (English)\"); null if unknown. */\n    81\texport function domainName(key: string): string | null {\n    82\t  const k = key.replace(/^F_(CE|gap)_/, \"\");\n    83\t  return DOMAIN_NAMES[k] ?? null;\n    84\t}\n    85\texport function domainKeys(m: Model, prefix = \"F_CE_\"): string[] {\n    86\t  return Object.keys(m.fingerprint ?? {}).filter((k) => k.startsWith(prefix) && k !== prefix.replace(/_$/, \"\"));\n    87\t}\n    88\t\n    89\t/** Nearest preceding rung in the same family whose stage is not post-trained. */\n    90\texport function basePairFor(bundle: Bundle, post: Model): Model | null {\n    91\t  const rungs = ladder(bundle, post.family).filter((r) => r.order < post.order && r.stage !== \"post-trained\");\n    92\t  return rungs.length ? rungs[rungs.length - 1] : null;\n    93\t}\n    94\t\n    95\t/** Default compare sibling (DESIGN §7.2): nearest preceding pre/base rung for post-trained, else next rung. */\n    96\texport function defaultCompare(bundle: Bundle, m: Model): Model | null {\n    97\t  const rungs = ladder(bundle, m.family);\n    98\t  if (m.stage === \"post-trained\") {\n    99\t    const prev = rungs.filter((r) => r.order < m.order && (r.stage === \"pre-anneal\" || r.stage === \"base\"));\n   100\t    if (prev.length) return prev[prev.length - 1];\n   101\t    return basePairFor(bundle, m);\n   102\t  }\n   103\t  const next = rungs.find((r) => r.order > m.order);\n   104\t  if (next) return next;\n   105\t  const prev = rungs.filter((r) => r.order < m.order);\n   106\t  return prev.length ? prev[prev.length - 1] : null;\n   107\t}\n   108\t\n   109\t/** Ranked list by overall desc, ties by family then order. Rank ties judged on the displayed 3-dp value. */\n   110\texport function ranked(bundle: Bundle): { model: Model; rank: number; tie: boolean }[] {\n   111\t  const ms = [...bundle.models].sort(compareOverall);\n   112\t  const disp = (m: Model) => (m.score === null ? null : m.score.toFixed(3));\n   113\t  const out: { model: Model; rank: number; tie: boolean }[] = [];\n   114\t  ms.forEach((m, i) => {\n   115\t    const prev = out[i - 1];\n   116\t    if (prev && disp(prev.model) === disp(m) && disp(m) !== null) {\n   117\t      out.push({ model: m, rank: prev.rank, tie: true });\n   118\t      prev.tie = true;\n   119\t    } else out.push({ model: m, rank: i + 1, tie: false });\n   120\t  });\n   121\t  return out;\n   122\t}\n   123\t\n   124\texport function compareOverall(a: Model, b: Model): number {\n   125\t  const sa = a.score ?? -Infinity;\n   126\t  const sb = b.score ?? -Infinity;\n   127\t  if (sb !== sa) return sb - sa;\n   128\t  if (a.family !== b.family) return a.family.localeCompare(b.family);\n   129\t  return a.order - b.order;\n   130\t}\n   131\t\n   132\texport function nAvailable(m: Model): number {\n   133\t  return SUB_KEYS.filter((k) => typeof m.subscores?.[k]?.score === \"number\").length;\n   134\t}\n   135\t\n   136\texport function subScoreOf(m: Model, k: SubKey): number | null {\n   137\t  return get(m, [\"subscores\", k, \"score\"]);\n   138\t}\n   139\t\n   140\t// ---------- computed specimens (§6.4) ----------\n   141\t\n   142\t/** The family with the most rungs that has both a pre-anneal and a post-trained rung. */\n   143\texport function specimenFamily(bundle: Bundle): string | null {\n   144\t  const fams = familiesOf(bundle)\n   145\t    .map((f) => ({ f, rungs: ladder(bundle, f) }))\n   146\t    .filter(({ rungs }) => rungs.some((r) => r.stage === \"pre-anneal\") && rungs.some((r) => r.stage === \"post-trained\"))\n   147\t    .sort((a, b) => b.rungs.length - a.rungs.length || a.f.localeCompare(b.f));\n   148\t  return fams.length ? fams[0].f : null;\n   149\t}\n   150\t\n   151\t/** A = lowest-order pre-anneal rung of the family; B = its highest-order post-trained rung. */\n   152\texport function specimenPair(bundle: Bundle, family = specimenFamily(bundle)): { A: Model; B: Model } | null {\n   153\t  if (!family) return null;\n   154\t  const rungs = ladder(bundle, family);\n   155\t  const A = rungs.find((r) => r.stage === \"pre-anneal\");\n   156\t  const posts = rungs.filter((r) => r.stage === \"post-trained\");\n   157\t  if (!A || !posts.length) return null;\n   158\t  return { A, B: posts[posts.length - 1] };\n   159\t}\n   160\t\n   161\t/** ± max |F_calib_gap| over pre-anneal models, with the model that sets it. */\n   162\texport function preAnnealBand(bundle: Bundle): { band: number; model: Model } | null {\n   163\t  let best: { band: number; model: Model } | null = null;\n   164\t  for (const m of bundle.models) {\n   165\t    if (m.stage !== \"pre-anneal\") continue;\n   166\t    const g = fp(m, \"F_calib_gap\");\n   167\t    if (g === null) continue;\n   168\t    if (!best || Math.abs(g) > best.band) best = { band: Math.abs(g), model: m };\n   169\t  }\n   170\t  return best;\n   171\t}\n   172\t\n   173\t/** The pre-anneal model with the highest roll_frac_H<0.1. */\n   174\texport function collapseSpecimen(bundle: Bundle): Model | null {\n   175\t  const c = bundle.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"pre-anneal\" && fp(m, \"roll_frac_H<0.1\") !== null);\n   176\t  c.sort((a, b) => fp(b, \"roll_frac_H<0.1\")! - fp(a, \"roll_frac_H<0.1\")!);\n   177\t  return c[0] ?? null;\n   178\t}\n   179\t\n   180\texport function memoValue(m: Model): number | null {\n   181\t  return fp(m, \"X_runfrac_wiki\") ?? fp(m, \"verbatim_run_frac_wiki\");\n   182\t}\n   183\t\n   184\t/**\n   185\t * Among families with a pre-anneal rung: the (last pre-anneal rung -> first following non-post rung) pair with\n   186\t * the largest rise in X_runfrac_wiki, plus the next post-trained rung.\n   187\t */\n   188\texport function memoTriple(bundle: Bundle): Model[] | null {\n   189\t  let best: { rise: number; trio: Model[] } | null = null;\n   190\t  for (const f of familiesOf(bundle)) {\n   191\t    const rungs = ladder(bundle, f);\n   192\t    const pres = rungs.filter((r) => r.stage === \"pre-anneal\");\n   193\t    if (!pres.length) continue;\n   194\t    const lastPre = pres[pres.length - 1];\n   195\t    const next = rungs.find((r) => r.order > lastPre.order && r.stage !== \"post-trained\" && r.stage !== \"pre-anneal\");\n   196\t    if (!next) continue;\n   197\t    const a = memoValue(lastPre);\n   198\t    const b = memoValue(next);\n   199\t    if (a === null || b === null) continue;\n   200\t    const post = rungs.find((r) => r.order > next.order && r.stage === \"post-trained\");\n   201\t    const trio = post ? [lastPre, next, post] : [lastPre, next];\n   202\t    if (!best || b - a > best.rise) best = { rise: b - a, trio };\n   203\t  }\n   204\t  return best?.trio ?? null;\n   205\t}\n   206\t\n   207\t/** The model with the most sub-scores strictly inside (0, 1); ties go to the overall closest to 0.5. */\n   208\texport function rampSpecimen(bundle: Bundle): Model | null {\n   209\t  const inside = (m: Model) => SUB_KEYS.filter((k) => {\n   210\t    const s = subScoreOf(m, k);\n   211\t    return s !== null && s > 0 && s < 1;\n   212\t  }).length;\n   213\t  const ms = bundle.models.filter((m) => m.score !== null);\n   214\t  ms.sort((a, b) => inside(b) - inside(a) || Math.abs(a.score! - 0.5) - Math.abs(b.score! - 0.5) || a.id.localeCompare(b.id));\n   215\t  return ms[0] ?? null;\n   216\t}\n   217\t\n   218\t/** Among pairs with |Δoverall| < 0.005, the pair with the largest L1 distance between sub-score vectors. */\n   219\texport function sameScorePair(bundle: Bundle): { a: Model; b: Model; l1: number } | null {\n   220\t  const ms = bundle.models.filter((m) => m.score !== null && nAvailable(m) === 4);\n   221\t  let best: { a: Model; b: Model; l1: number } | null = null;\n   222\t  for (let i = 0; i < ms.length; i++)\n   223\t    for (let j = i + 1; j < ms.length; j++) {\n   224\t      const a = ms[i];\n   225\t      const b = ms[j];\n   226\t      if (Math.abs(a.score! - b.score!) >= 0.005) continue;\n   227\t      const l1 = SUB_KEYS.reduce((s, k) => s + Math.abs(subScoreOf(a, k)! - subScoreOf(b, k)!), 0);\n   228\t      if (!best || l1 > best.l1) best = { a, b, l1 };\n   229\t    }\n   230\t  if (best && compareOverall(best.a, best.b) > 0) best = { a: best.b, b: best.a, l1: best.l1 };\n   231\t  return best;\n   232\t}\n   233\t\n   234\t/** Fallback when no same-score pair: A, the base with lowest memo score, the base with lowest prior score. */\n   235\texport function recipeTrio(bundle: Bundle): Model[] | null {\n   236\t  const sp = specimenPair(bundle);\n   237\t  const bases = bundle.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"base\");\n   238\t  // ties on the clipped score (several bases sit at 0) go to the raw value furthest past the 0-anchor\n   239\t  const unclipped = (m: Model, k: SubKey) => {\n   240\t    const v = get(m, [\"subscores\", k, \"value\"]);\n   241\t    const [good, bad] = RAMPS[k];\n   242\t    return v === null ? Infinity : (v - bad) / (good - bad);\n   243\t  };\n   244\t  const lowest = (k: SubKey) =>\n   245\t    bases\n   246\t      .filter((m) => subScoreOf(m, k) !== null)\n   247\t      .sort((a, b) => subScoreOf(a, k)! - subScoreOf(b, k)! || unclipped(a, k) - unclipped(b, k))[0];\n   248\t  const lm = lowest(\"memo\");\n   249\t  const lp = lowest(\"prior\");\n   250\t  if (!sp || !lm || !lp) return null;\n   251\t  return [sp.A, lm, lp];\n   252\t}\n   253\t\n   254\texport function rangeOf(xs: (number | null)[]): [number, number] | null {\n   255\t  const v = xs.filter((x): x is number => x !== null && Number.isFinite(x));\n   256\t  if (!v.length) return null;\n   257\t  return [Math.min(...v), Math.max(...v)];\n   258\t}\n   259\t\n   260\texport function median(xs: number[]): number | null {\n   261\t  if (!xs.length) return null;\n   262\t  const s = [...xs].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n   263\t  const m = s.length >> 1;\n   264\t  return s.length % 2 ? s[m] : (s[m - 1] + s[m]) / 2;\n   265\t}\n     1\t/**\n     2\t * Findings F1–F6 (DESIGN §7.4): every claim is computed from the bundle and has a predicate.\n     3\t * If a predicate fails, the page shows a Data note saying what no longer holds. Pure functions: tested in\n     4\t * src/test/findings.test.ts (snapshot of computed ranges).\n     5\t */\n     6\timport { fp, get } from \"./data/bundle\";\n     7\timport { basePairFor, collapseSpecimen, ladder, median, memoTriple, memoValue, preAnnealBand, specimenFamily, specimenPair } from \"./data/derive\";\n     8\timport { SURNAME_FRAMES } from \"./data/method\";\n     9\timport type { Bundle, Model } from \"./data/types\";\n    10\t\n    11\t/** Definitions used by the checks (not measurements). */\n    12\texport const FINDING_DEFS = {\n    13\t  /** SPEC: pre-anneal checkpoints sit within ±0.02 of 0 */\n    14\t  PRE_BAND_MAX: 0.02,\n    15\t  /** tolerance for non-monotone steps inside one stage (OLMo-2 anneal rungs) */\n    16\t  STAGE_TOL: 0.005,\n    17\t  /** a base counts as \"clean\" for the surname range when its overall score is at least this */\n    18\t  CLEAN_BASE_MIN_OVERALL: 0.85,\n    19\t  /** a post-trained rung supports F6 (gap) when gap_hiH falls by more than this vs its base pair, nats */\n    20\t  F6_GAP_SUPPORT: 0.25,\n    21\t  /** a post-trained rung supports F6 (rate) when the s>10 rate rises by at least this fraction */\n    22\t  F6_RATE_SUPPORT: 0.25,\n    23\t  /** a base counts as ≈ 0 on gap_hiH within this many nats */\n    24\t  BASE_HIH_ZERO: 0.15,\n    25\t};\n    26\t\n    27\texport interface Check {\n    28\t  ok: boolean;\n    29\t  /** what no longer holds, when !ok */\n    30\t  note: string;\n    31\t}\n    32\t\n    33\tconst num = (x: number | null | undefined): x is number => typeof x === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(x);\n    34\t\n    35\t// ---------- F1 calibration ladder ----------\n    36\texport interface F1 {\n    37\t  band: { band: number; model: Model } | null;\n    38\t  family: string | null;\n    39\t  /** last rung of each stage, post-trained rungs individually, in order */\n    40\t  steps: Model[];\n    41\t  checks: { band: Check; monotone: Check; domains: Check };\n    42\t  preDomainMin: { model: Model; value: number }[];\n    43\t}\n    44\t\n    45\t/** Last rung of each pre/anneal/base stage, plus every post-trained rung (SFT → DPO → RLVR are separate steps). */\n    46\texport function stageSteps(rungs: Model[]): Model[] {\n    47\t  const out: Model[] = [];\n    48\t  rungs.forEach((r, i) => {\n    49\t    const next = rungs[i + 1];\n    50\t    if (r.stage === \"post-trained\" || !next || next.stage !== r.stage) out.push(r);\n    51\t  });\n    52\t  return out;\n    53\t}\n    54\t\n    55\texport function f1(b: Bundle): F1 {\n    56\t  const band = preAnnealBand(b);\n    57\t  const family = specimenFamily(b);\n    58\t  const rungs = family ? ladder(b, family) : [];\n    59\t  const steps = stageSteps(rungs);\n    60\t  const g = (m: Model) => fp(m, \"F_calib_gap\");\n    61\t  const bandOk = !!band && band.band <= FINDING_DEFS.PRE_BAND_MAX;\n    62\t  // monotone across stage steps; inside a stage allow STAGE_TOL rises\n    63\t  const bad: string[] = [];\n    64\t  for (let i = 1; i < steps.length; i++) {\n    65\t    const a = g(steps[i - 1]);\n    66\t    const c = g(steps[i]);\n    67\t    if (num(a) && num(c) && c > a) bad.push(`${steps[i - 1].label} → ${steps[i].label}`);\n    68\t  }\n    69\t  for (let i = 1; i < rungs.length; i++) {\n    70\t    if (rungs[i].stage !== rungs[i - 1].stage || rungs[i].stage === \"post-trained\") continue;\n    71\t    const a = g(rungs[i - 1]);\n    72\t    const c = g(rungs[i]);\n    73\t    if (num(a) && num(c) && c - a > FINDING_DEFS.STAGE_TOL) bad.push(`${rungs[i - 1].label} → ${rungs[i].label} (inside a stage)`);\n    74\t  }\n    75\t  const pres = b.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"pre-anneal\");\n    76\t  const preDomainMin = pres.map((m) => ({ model: m, value: fp(m, \"F_gap_domain_min\") })).filter((x): x is { model: Model; value: number } => num(x.value));\n    77\t  const domBad = preDomainMin.filter((x) => x.value < -FINDING_DEFS.PRE_BAND_MAX);\n    78\t  return {\n    79\t    band,\n    80\t    family,\n    81\t    steps,\n    82\t    preDomainMin,\n    83\t    checks: {\n    84\t      band: {\n    85\t        ok: bandOk,\n    86\t        note: band\n    87\t          ? `the widest pre-anneal gap (${band.model.label}) exceeds ±${FINDING_DEFS.PRE_BAND_MAX} nats`\n    88\t          : \"no pre-anneal checkpoint with a post-cutoff gap in this bundle\",\n    89\t      },\n    90\t      monotone: {\n    91\t        ok: steps.length > 1 && bad.length === 0,\n    92\t        note: steps.length > 1 ? `the gap does not fall monotonically along ${family}: ${bad.join(\"; \")}` : \"no ladder with both pre-anneal and post-trained rungs\",\n    93\t      },\n    94\t      domains: {\n    95\t        ok: domBad.length === 0,\n    96\t        note: `a pre-anneal checkpoint has a post-cutoff domain gap below −${FINDING_DEFS.PRE_BAND_MAX}: ${domBad.map((x) => x.model.label).join(\", \")}`,\n    97\t      },\n    98\t    },\n    99\t  };\n   100\t}\n   101\t\n   102\t// ---------- F2 annealed is not one thing ----------\n   103\texport interface F2 {\n   104\t  memo: Model[] | null; // last pre-anneal, first annealed, next post\n   105\t  priorFamily: string | null;\n   106\t  priorPre: Model | null;\n   107\t  priorAnneal: Model[];\n   108\t  priorRange: [number, number] | null;\n   109\t  checks: { memo: Check; prior: Check; different: Check };\n   110\t}\n   111\t\n   112\texport function f2(b: Bundle): F2 {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba site/src/content/measure.md && nl -ba site/src/content/about.md && nl -ba site/src/content/findings.md | sed -n '80,115p' && nl -ba site/src/pages/Measure.tsx && nl -ba site/src/pages/Methodology.tsx\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba site/src/content/measure.md && nl -ba site/src/content/about.md && nl -ba site/src/content/findings.md | sed -n '80,115p' && nl -ba site/src/pages/Measure.tsx && nl -ba site/src/pages/Methodology.tsx\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Measure your model\n     2\t\n     3\tEvery checkpoint on the board was measured with the scripts in `docs/` of this repository, on the same fixed text. To add one, run the same passes on your checkpoint, run the analysis scripts, export a bundle with `tools/export_bundle.py` and open a pull request. This page walks through each step.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Environment {#environment}\n     6\t\n     7\t- **Hardware.** The reference machine is one node with 8×B200 (about 180 GB each). Smaller checkpoints run on fewer or smaller GPUs; the HF path spreads layers over every visible GPU with `device_map=\"auto\"`, and the vLLM path uses tensor parallelism.\n     8\t- **Python.** 3.10 or newer with `torch`, `transformers`, `numpy` and `datasets` (the HF path), plus `vllm` with the V1 engine for the vLLM path. The analysis scripts need only `numpy`.\n     9\t- **Precision.** Weights load in bf16 unless you ask otherwise; all distribution statistics are computed in float32 from the full logits row. Record any change (FP8 quantisation, dequantisation) in your submission.\n    10\t- **Paths.** The script defaults point at `/models/varentropy/...` on the original machine. Pass `--corpus` and `--out` explicitly. Two analysis scripts still have the path in their source: `vh_selfboot.py` (`corpus_path`) and `vh_tail.py` (`res`). Edit those two lines to point at your corpus file and results directory.\n    11\t\n    12\t## Corpora {#corpora}\n    13\t\n    14\tEvery model must read exactly the same documents, so download the published corpus files rather than rebuilding them. They live next to the data bundles, under `corpus/` in the bench's data bucket, with a `SHA256SUMS` file; check your copies against it before measuring. All four teacher-forced corpora are JSON files of the form `{\"tf\": [{\"idx\", \"set\", \"text\"}, ...], \"openers\": [...]}`.\n    15\t\n    16\t| file (flag) | pass | contents |\n    17\t|---|---|---|\n    18\t| `pile10k_sel.json` (`--corpus`) | `tf`, own-sample openers | 400 Pile documents plus 64 openers, a fixed seeded selection from NeelNanda/pile-10k |\n    19\t| `wiki_gh.json` (`--extra-corpus tf_wiki=PATH`) | `tf_wiki` | 150 Wikipedia and 60 GitHub documents |\n    20\t| `uncheatable_2026_04.json` (`--extra-corpus tf_fresh=PATH`) | `tf_fresh` | 900 post-cutoff documents in 15 categories (UncheatableEval 2026-04) |\n    21\t| `weird.json` (`--extra-corpus tf_weird=PATH`) | `tf_weird` | 199 documents from 12 rare Pile genres |\n    22\t| `names.json` (`--names-probe`) | surname probe | `{\"surnames\": [[name, count], ...]}` from the 2010 US Census, most common first |\n    23\t\n    24\t{{block:corpora}}\n    25\t\n    26\tIf `--corpus` points at a file that does not exist, `vh_score.py` builds the Pile selection from the pile-10k Arrow file given by `--arrow` (seed 0, documents of at least 400 characters) and writes it there. Only do this to reproduce the published file; a different selection makes your numbers incomparable.\n    27\t\n    28\tThe `tf_fresh` texts post-date the training cutoff of every model in v1. If your checkpoint's data extends past April 2026, its `tf_fresh` pass is no longer post-cutoff; say so in the pull request.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Run the passes: HF transformers {#run-hf}\n    31\t\n    32\tUse `vh_score.py` for dense checkpoints and MoE checkpoints that transformers loads natively.\n    33\t\n    34\t```bash\n    35\tpython docs/vh_score.py --model /path/to/checkpoint --name my-model \\\n    36\t  --out results --corpus corpus/pile10k_sel.json \\\n    37\t  --extra-corpus tf_wiki=corpus/wiki_gh.json \\\n    38\t  --extra-corpus tf_fresh=corpus/uncheatable_2026_04.json \\\n    39\t  --extra-corpus tf_weird=corpus/weird.json \\\n    40\t  --names-probe corpus/names.json --max-mem-gib 165\n    41\t```\n    42\t\n    43\tUseful flags:\n    44\t\n    45\t- `--max-mem-gib` caps the memory per GPU used for weights, leaving room for full-vocabulary logits.\n    46\t- `--trust-remote-code` for custom-code checkpoints; `--arch deepseek_v3` forces the native DeepSeek-V3 class for Kimi K2 and DeepSeek-V3-architecture checkpoints that ship custom code.\n    47\t- `--quantize-fp8` quantises a bf16 checkpoint to block-128 FP8 at load; `--fp8-dequantize` does the reverse for FP8 checkpoints the quantiser cannot wrap.\n    48\t- `--experts-impl` selects the transformers experts implementation for MoE models.\n    49\t- `--skip-tf` / `--skip-rollouts` rerun only part of a model. Extra corpora whose `.npz` already exists are skipped, so an interrupted run can be resumed.\n    50\t\n    51\t## Run the passes: vLLM {#run-vllm}\n    52\t\n    53\tUse `vh_score_vllm.py` for checkpoints transformers cannot load efficiently, such as FP8 custom-code MoE. It writes the same files as the HF path. Exact per-step statistics come from the logits processor in `vh_lp.py`; teacher forcing replays each document with the real token forced through the logits mask. vLLM loads the processor by module name in its worker processes, so put `docs/` on `PYTHONPATH`.\n    54\t\n    55\t```bash\n    56\tPYTHONPATH=docs python docs/vh_score_vllm.py --model /path/to/checkpoint --name my-model \\\n    57\t  --out results --corpus corpus/pile10k_sel.json --tp 8 --trust-remote-code \\\n    58\t  --extra-corpus tf_wiki=corpus/wiki_gh.json \\\n    59\t  --extra-corpus tf_fresh=corpus/uncheatable_2026_04.json \\\n    60\t  --extra-corpus tf_weird=corpus/weird.json \\\n    61\t  --names-probe corpus/names.json --rollout-steps 1024\n    62\t```\n    63\t\n    64\t- `--prepend-bos` adds the BOS token when the tokenizer does not (DeepSeek V4 needs it). Check that your tokenizer's first token on the corpus is BOS on either path.\n    65\t- `--rollout-steps 1024` is what the FP8 giants used in v1; the default and the reference value elsewhere is 1536. A shorter rollout lowers the collapse statistics' exposure and must be declared.\n    66\t- `--gpu-mem`, `--max-num-seqs`, `--max-model-len` and `--max-num-batched-tokens` are passed through to vLLM.\n    67\t- Prefix caching is disabled on purpose: each request must produce its own statistics.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Expected runtime {#runtime}\n    70\t\n    71\tThe work is fixed: about 290k Pile tokens, 1.3M post-cutoff tokens, the Wikipedia/GitHub and rare-genre sets, 64 sampled sequences of 1536 steps, 6 × 2,000 surname scorings and 6 × 16 sampled surname lists. Every position keeps the full vocabulary distribution, so throughput is lower than for an ordinary evaluation run. Runtime therefore scales with parameter count and vocabulary size: on the reference node, expect minutes to tens of minutes per pass for a small dense model and hours in total for the largest MoE checkpoints. On the vLLM path teacher forcing costs more than a single forward pass per document, because each document is replayed step by step. The load time is written to `run.json` in the result directory, and every phase logs its own timing.\n    72\t\n    73\t## Analyse {#analyse}\n    74\t\n    75\tRun in this order, from the repository root. Each script reads the per-model directories under `results/` and writes into `results/analysis/`.\n    76\t\n    77\t```bash\n    78\tpython docs/vh_analyze.py results --all --corpus corpus/pile10k_sel.json   # fingerprints.json\n    79\tpython docs/vh_memo.py results my-model                                    # memo_<model>.json\n    80\tpython docs/vh_selfboot.py results                                         # selfboot.json\n    81\tpython docs/vh_tail.py                                                     # tail_realised.json\n    82\tpython docs/vh_names_table.py results                                      # names_summary.json, weird_summary.json\n    83\tpython docs/vh_truebase.py results/analysis/fingerprints.json              # truebase_scores.json\n    84\t```\n    85\t\n    86\t`vh_truebase.py` prints the four sub-scores and the overall score; these are the numbers the leaderboard will show. `vh_report.py` renders a standalone HTML report and is not needed for a submission.\n    87\t\n    88\t## Export {#export}\n    89\t\n    90\tFirst add your checkpoint to the model registry `tools/models.json`, keyed by its result-directory name: its `family`, `stage` (one of pre-anneal, anneal, base, post-trained), a short `label` and its `order` in the family ladder. Models that are not in the registry are skipped by the export (`--strict` makes that an error). Then export and test:\n    91\t\n    92\t```bash\n    93\tpython tools/export_bundle.py results/analysis --out data/\n    94\tcd site && npm test\n    95\t```\n    96\t\n    97\t`export_bundle.py` merges the analysis files into one bundle in the schema below, carries over every model of the latest bundle that you did not re-measure, writes it as `bench_vN.json` and updates `manifest.json`. Use `--dry-run` first to see the summary without writing anything. `npm test` checks the bundle against the data contract and recomputes every overall score from the fingerprint with the published ramps; a mismatch fails the test. To preview, run `npm run dev` in `site/` and open your model's page.\n    98\t\n    99\t## Submit {#submit}\n   100\t\n   101\tOpen a pull request that adds your entries to a new `bench_vN.json` and lists it in `data/manifest.json`. Do not edit a published bundle; bundles are append-only so that every number ever shown on the site stays reproducible. Include in the pull request description:\n   102\t\n   103\t- the checkpoint id (a Hugging Face repo id and revision, or a public download location), its family, stage and position in the family ladder;\n   104\t- the commit of this repository whose scripts you ran, and the exact command lines;\n   105\t- hardware, precision, and every flag that differs from the defaults (FP8, rollout steps, `--prepend-bos`);\n   106\t- the output of `vh_truebase.py` for your model;\n   107\t- a passing `npm test`.\n   108\t\n   109\tMaintainers may rerun some passes on publicly downloadable checkpoints before merging, and will ask in the pull request if a number does not reproduce.\n   110\t\n   111\t## Data contract {#contract}\n   112\t\n   113\t{{block:datacontract}}\n     1\t# About\n     2\t\n     3\tTrue Base Bench is a public, permanent benchmark that measures how far a language-model checkpoint sits from a pre-anneal pretraining base: a checkpoint from the long constant-learning-rate phase of pretraining, before the anneal, mid-training mixes and any post-training. It is built and maintained by [Anima Labs](https://github.com/anima-research).\n     4\t\n     5\t## What it is for {#purpose}\n     6\t\n     7\t\"Base model\" has stopped meaning one thing. Released bases are often annealed on curated, instruction-heavy or synthetic mixes, distilled from a larger model, or both. For research that depends on a model trained toward the data and nothing else (interpretability, studies of the pretraining distribution, simulation, uses of base-model sampling that rely on its full diversity), the label on the checkpoint is not enough.\n     8\t\n     9\tThe bench gives that question a measurement. It does not score capability, and a higher score is not better in general: a score of 1 means the checkpoint is indistinguishable from a pre-anneal base on the four features measured, nothing more. Whether that is what you want depends on what you are using the model for.\n    10\t\n    11\t## How it works, briefly {#how}\n    12\t\n    13\tEvery model reads the same fixed text, and every feature is a statistic of its own next-token distribution: how well its expected surprise matches its realised surprise on text written after its training cutoff, how much of Wikipedia it recites verbatim, and how often it writes chat or instruction markers when asked to write from nothing. Four sub-scores, each a clipped linear ramp between a true-base anchor and a post-trained anchor, are averaged into the overall score. A surname-distribution test, tail statistics and lexical statistics of the model's own samples are shown alongside. [Methodology](/methodology) defines each of these precisely; [Findings](/findings) gives the results.\n    14\t\n    15\t## Data and reproducibility {#data}\n    16\t\n    17\t- All numbers on the site are read from a versioned data bundle (`bench_vN.json`, listed in `manifest.json`), served from public object storage with a copy in the repository. No number is typed into a page.\n    18\t- The measurement and analysis scripts are in `docs/` in the repository, unchanged from the runs that produced the bundle. [Measure your model](/measure) gives the exact commands.\n    19\t- The overall score is recomputable from the fingerprint in the bundle; the site's test suite checks this for every model.\n    20\t- Bundles are append-only. A new measurement, a new model or a change of method produces a new bundle version; earlier versions stay available so that cited numbers remain checkable.\n    21\t\n    22\t## Maintenance {#maintenance}\n    23\t\n    24\tThe bench is maintained by Anima Labs. New checkpoints are added through pull requests that include a bundle produced with the published scripts (see [Measure your model](/measure)), and by the maintainers as notable checkpoints are released. Changes to the method, such as new anchors, a new post-cutoff corpus or a new feature, are made only in a new bundle version, described in the pull request that introduces it; a model's score is always reported against a named bundle version.\n    25\t\n    26\tThe post-cutoff corpus has a date. Once checkpoints trained on data from after that date appear, the corpus has to be replaced by a newer one, in a new bundle version, with the existing models re-measured on it.\n    27\t\n    28\tCorrections, questions and submissions go to the issue tracker of the repository at [github.com/anima-research](https://github.com/anima-research).\n    29\t\n    30\t## Citation {#citation}\n    31\t\n    32\tIf you use the bench or its data, cite the bundle version you used. The entry below is for the bundle this page loaded, {{val:bundle}}; earlier versions stay listed in `manifest.json`.\n    33\t\n    34\t{{block:citation}}\n    35\t\n    36\t## Acknowledgements {#acknowledgements}\n    37\t\n    38\tThe surname test follows a proposal by Gwern Branwen. The post-cutoff text comes from UncheatableEval, the Pile selection from NeelNanda/pile-10k, and the surname reference from the 2010 US Census surname file. The anchors were set using checkpoints released by Arcee AI (Trinity) and the Allen Institute for AI (OLMo-2), whose publication of intermediate checkpoints makes a measurement like this possible.\n    80\t\n    81\t## Tail {#f6}\n    82\t\n    83\t{{block:f6-claim}}\n    84\t\n    85\tOn post-cutoff text, post-training raises the share of real tokens the model found near-impossible: surprisal above 10 nats, a probability below about 1 in 22,000. The paired chart below shows every base / post-trained pair, including the ones where the rise is small.\n    86\t\n    87\tThe over-confidence concentrates where the model is uncertain. At positions with H > 4 nats, bases have a gap near zero, while most post-trained models are well below it; the exceptions are named in the claim above and visible in the strip. Post-training does not just sharpen confident predictions; it removes the hedging from uncertain ones.\n    88\t\n    89\t{{block:f6-detail}}\n    90\t\n    91\t{{block:tail-arrows}}\n    92\t\n    93\t{{block:tail-strip}}\n    94\t\n    95\t{{block:f6-readoff}}\n    96\t\n    97\t## Every family, in stage order {#ladders}\n    98\t\n    99\tEach family's rungs in stage order, for a selectable metric (calibration gap, few-shot surname KL, top-1 ECE), on a shared axis.\n   100\t\n   101\t{{block:ladders}}\n     1\timport { useMemo } from \"react\";\n     2\timport source from \"../content/measure.md?raw\";\n     3\timport { ReadingPage } from \"../components/ReadingPage\";\n     4\timport { useBundle } from \"../data/BundleContext\";\n     5\timport { describe, FIELD_DOCS } from \"../data/glossary\";\n     6\timport { Chevron } from \"../components/Plate\";\n     7\timport type { Bundle } from \"../data/types\";\n     8\timport { liveBase } from \"../data/load\";\n     9\timport { CopyButton } from \"../components/CopyButton\";\n    10\t\n    11\t/** The fixed measurement inputs, published under corpus/ in the data bucket (tools/upload_data.py --corpus-dir). */\n    12\texport const CORPUS_FILES = [\"pile10k_sel.json\", \"wiki_gh.json\", \"uncheatable_2026_04.json\", \"weird.json\", \"names.json\"] as const;\n    13\t\n    14\tfunction Corpora() {\n    15\t  const base = liveBase();\n    16\t  const root = base ? `${base}/corpus` : \"<data bucket>/corpus\";\n    17\t  const script = [\n    18\t    \"mkdir -p corpus && cd corpus\",\n    19\t    `for f in ${CORPUS_FILES.join(\" \")} SHA256SUMS; do`,\n    20\t    `  curl -fSLO \"${root}/$f\"`,\n    21\t    \"done\",\n    22\t    \"sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS && cd ..\",\n    23\t  ].join(\"\\n\");\n    24\t  return (\n    25\t    <div className=\"corpora\">\n    26\t      {base ? (\n    27\t        <ul className=\"small\">\n    28\t          {[...CORPUS_FILES, \"SHA256SUMS\"].map((f) => (\n    29\t            <li key={f}>\n    30\t              <a className=\"mono\" href={`${root}/${f}`}>\n    31\t                {f}\n    32\t              </a>\n    33\t            </li>\n    34\t          ))}\n    35\t        </ul>\n    36\t      ) : (\n    37\t        <p className=\"small ink2\">\n    38\t          This build has no data bucket configured (VITE_DATA_BASE_URL), so the download links cannot be shown here; the files are\n    39\t          under <span className=\"mono\">corpus/</span> next to the published bundles.\n    40\t        </p>\n    41\t      )}\n    42\t      <pre className=\"code\">\n    43\t        <code>{script}</code>\n    44\t        <CopyButton className=\"copy\" text={script} />\n    45\t      </pre>\n    46\t    </div>\n    47\t  );\n    48\t}\n    49\t\n    50\tinterface Row {\n    51\t  key: string;\n    52\t  type: string;\n    53\t  nullable: number;\n    54\t  total: number;\n    55\t}\n    56\t\n    57\tfunction typeOf(v: unknown): string {\n    58\t  if (v === null || v === undefined) return \"null\";\n    59\t  if (Array.isArray(v)) return \"array\";\n    60\t  return typeof v;\n    61\t}\n    62\t\n    63\t/** Field inventory of the loaded bundle: per-model keys with types and how often they are null. */\n    64\texport function contractRows(bundle: Bundle): Row[] {\n    65\t  const rows = new Map<string, { types: Set<string>; nulls: number }>();\n    66\t  const visit = (prefix: string, v: unknown, depth: number) => {\n    67\t    const t = typeOf(v);\n    68\t    const r = rows.get(prefix) ?? { types: new Set<string>(), nulls: 0 };\n    69\t    if (t === \"null\") r.nulls++;\n    70\t    else r.types.add(t);\n    71\t    rows.set(prefix, r);\n    72\t    if (t === \"object\" && depth < 2 && prefix !== \"fingerprint\" && prefix !== \"weird\")\n    73\t      for (const [k, x] of Object.entries(v as object)) visit(`${prefix}.${k}`, x, depth + 1);\n    74\t  };\n    75\t  for (const m of bundle.models) for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(m)) visit(k, v, 0);\n    76\t  return Array.from(rows.entries()).map(([key, r]) => ({ key, type: Array.from(r.types).join(\" | \") || \"null\", nullable: r.nulls, total: bundle.models.length }));\n    77\t}\n    78\t\n    79\tfunction DataContract() {\n    80\t  const bundle = useBundle();\n    81\t  const rows = contractRows(bundle);\n    82\t  return (\n    83\t    <details className=\"disclosure\">\n    84\t      <summary>\n    85\t        <Chevron /> Fields of models[] in the loaded bundle ({rows.length})\n    86\t      </summary>\n    87\t      <div className=\"tbl-scroll\">\n    88\t        <table className=\"tbl\">\n    89\t          <thead>\n    90\t            <tr>\n    91\t              <th scope=\"col\">field</th>\n    92\t              <th scope=\"col\">type</th>\n    93\t              <th scope=\"col\" className=\"n\">\n    94\t                null in\n    95\t              </th>\n    96\t              <th scope=\"col\">description</th>\n    97\t            </tr>\n    98\t          </thead>\n    99\t          <tbody>\n   100\t            {rows.map((r) => (\n   101\t              <tr key={r.key}>\n   102\t                <td className=\"mono small\">{r.key}</td>\n   103\t                <td className=\"small\">{r.type}</td>\n   104\t                <td className=\"n small\">\n   105\t                  {r.nullable} / {r.total}\n   106\t                </td>\n   107\t                <td className=\"small ink2\">{FIELD_DOCS[r.key] ?? describe(r.key.split(\".\").pop() ?? \"\") ?? \"—\"}</td>\n   108\t              </tr>\n   109\t            ))}\n   110\t          </tbody>\n   111\t        </table>\n   112\t      </div>\n   113\t    </details>\n   114\t  );\n   115\t}\n   116\t\n   117\texport default function Measure() {\n   118\t  const blocks = useMemo(() => ({ corpora: () => <Corpora />, datacontract: () => <DataContract /> }), []);\n   119\t  return <ReadingPage source={source} blocks={blocks} docTitle=\"Measure your model · True Base Bench\" />;\n   120\t}\n     1\timport { useMemo, type ReactNode } from \"react\";\n     2\timport source from \"../content/methodology.md?raw\";\n     3\timport { ReadingPage } from \"../components/ReadingPage\";\n     4\timport { useBundle } from \"../data/BundleContext\";\n     5\timport { RAMPS, SOURCES, SUB_META } from \"../data/method\";\n     6\timport { SUB_KEYS } from \"../data/types\";\n     7\timport { Num } from \"../components/Num\";\n     8\timport { preAnnealBand, specimenPair } from \"../data/derive\";\n     9\timport { fp, path } from \"../data/bundle\";\n    10\timport type { MethodologyValue } from \"../content/slots\";\n    11\timport type { Fmt } from \"../lib/numfmt\";\n    12\t\n    13\tconst WHY: Record<string, string> = {\n    14\t  calib: \"pre-anneal checkpoints sit near 0; post-training pushes the gap negative\",\n    15\t  spread: \"pre-anneal checkpoints are near-zero in every post-cutoff domain\",\n    16\t  memo: \"annealing on up-weighted sources raises the share of Wikipedia recited verbatim\",\n    17\t  prior: \"instruction data in the mix shows up as chat markers in bare-BOS samples\",\n    18\t};\n    19\t\n    20\tfunction AnchorsTable() {\n    21\t  const bundle = useBundle();\n    22\t  const ramps = (bundle.reference.ramps as Record<string, [number, number]> | null | undefined) ?? RAMPS;\n    23\t  const f = (k: string): Fmt => (SUB_META[k as keyof typeof SUB_META].unit === \"share\" ? \"pct\" : k === \"calib\" ? \"signed\" : \"num3\");\n    24\t  return (\n    25\t    <div className=\"tbl-scroll\">\n    26\t      <table className=\"tbl\">\n    27\t        <caption>Ramp anchors ({bundle.reference.ramps ? \"from reference.ramps\" : \"definitions in src/data/method.ts, mirroring docs/vh_truebase.py\"}).</caption>\n    28\t        <thead>\n    29\t          <tr>\n    30\t            <th scope=\"col\" className=\"nw\">\n    31\t              sub-score\n    32\t            </th>\n    33\t            <th scope=\"col\">feature (fallback)</th>\n    34\t            <th scope=\"col\" className=\"n nw\">\n    35\t              1 at\n    36\t            </th>\n    37\t            <th scope=\"col\" className=\"n nw\">\n    38\t              0 at\n    39\t            </th>\n    40\t            <th scope=\"col\">why this anchor</th>\n    41\t          </tr>\n    42\t        </thead>\n    43\t        <tbody>\n    44\t          {SUB_KEYS.map((k) => (\n    45\t            <tr key={k}>\n    46\t              <th scope=\"row\" style={{ background: \"none\" }}>\n    47\t                {SUB_META[k].name}\n    48\t              </th>\n    49\t              <td className=\"mono small\">{SOURCES[k].join(\" → \")}</td>\n    50\t              <td className=\"n\">\n    51\t                <Num path={`method.RAMPS.${k}[0]`} value={ramps[k]?.[0] ?? null} fmt={f(k)} def unit={false} />\n    52\t              </td>\n    53\t              <td className=\"n\">\n    54\t                <Num path={`method.RAMPS.${k}[1]`} value={ramps[k]?.[1] ?? null} fmt={f(k)} def unit={false} />\n    55\t              </td>\n    56\t              <td className=\"small ink2\">\n    57\t                {WHY[k]}\n    58\t                {k === \"calib\" ? \"; positive gaps are clipped to 1\" : \"\"}\n    59\t              </td>\n    60\t            </tr>\n    61\t          ))}\n    62\t        </tbody>\n    63\t      </table>\n    64\t    </div>\n    65\t  );\n    66\t}\n    67\t\n    68\texport default function Methodology() {\n    69\t  const bundle = useBundle();\n    70\t  const sp = specimenPair(bundle);\n    71\t  const blocks = useMemo(() => ({ anchors: () => <AnchorsTable /> }), []);\n    72\t  const values = useMemo<Record<MethodologyValue, () => ReactNode>>(() => {\n    73\t    const band = preAnnealBand(bundle);\n    74\t    const pre = bundle.models.filter((m) => m.stage === \"pre-anneal\" && fp(m, \"F_gap_domain_min\") !== null);\n    75\t    const domMin = pre.reduce<(typeof pre)[number] | null>((a, m) => (!a || fp(m, \"F_gap_domain_min\")! < fp(a, \"F_gap_domain_min\")! ? m : a), null);\n    76\t    const name = (m: { family: string; label: string }) => `${m.family} ${m.label}`;\n    77\t    return {\n    78\t      \"pre-band\": () =>\n    79\t        band ? <Num path={path(band.model, \"fingerprint.F_calib_gap\")} value={band.band} fmt=\"nats\" derived=\"max |F_calib_gap| over pre-anneal checkpoints\" /> : <>—</>,\n    80\t      \"pre-domain-min\": () => (domMin ? <Num path={path(domMin, \"fingerprint.F_gap_domain_min\")} fmt=\"gap\" /> : <>—</>),\n    81\t      \"spec-a\": () => <>{sp ? name(sp.A) : \"—\"}</>,\n    82\t      \"spec-b\": () => <>{sp ? name(sp.B) : \"—\"}</>,\n    83\t      \"spec-a-gap\": () => (sp ? <Num path={path(sp.A, \"fingerprint.F_calib_gap\")} fmt=\"gap\" /> : <>—</>),\n    84\t      \"spec-b-gap\": () => (sp ? <Num path={path(sp.B, \"fingerprint.F_calib_gap\")} fmt=\"gap\" /> : <>—</>),\n    85\t    };\n    86\t  }, [bundle, sp]);\n    87\t  return (\n    88\t    <ReadingPage\n    89\t      source={source}\n    90\t      blocks={blocks}\n    91\t      values={values}\n    92\t      docTitle=\"Methodology · True Base Bench\"\n    93\t      before={\n    94\t        sp ? (\n    95\t          <p className=\"small ink2\" style={{ marginBottom: 16 }}>\n    96\t            Specimens: A = {sp.A.label} vs B = {sp.B.label} of {sp.A.family} (computed: the family with the most rungs that has both a pre-anneal and a\n    97\t            post-trained rung).\n    98\t          </p>\n    99\t        ) : null\n   100\t      }\n   101\t    />\n   102\t  );\n   103\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba site/src/content/measure.md | sed -n '1,74p' && nl -ba site/src/components/Num.tsx && nl -ba site/src/components/Chart.tsx && nl -ba site/src/components/Chrome.tsx\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba site/src/content/measure.md | sed -n '1,74p' && nl -ba site/src/components/Num.tsx && nl -ba site/src/components/Chart.tsx && nl -ba site/src/components/Chrome.tsx\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Measure your model\n     2\t\n     3\tEvery checkpoint on the board was measured with the scripts in `docs/` of this repository, on the same fixed text. To add one, run the same passes on your checkpoint, run the analysis scripts, export a bundle with `tools/export_bundle.py` and open a pull request. This page walks through each step.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Environment {#environment}\n     6\t\n     7\t- **Hardware.** The reference machine is one node with 8×B200 (about 180 GB each). Smaller checkpoints run on fewer or smaller GPUs; the HF path spreads layers over every visible GPU with `device_map=\"auto\"`, and the vLLM path uses tensor parallelism.\n     8\t- **Python.** 3.10 or newer with `torch`, `transformers`, `numpy` and `datasets` (the HF path), plus `vllm` with the V1 engine for the vLLM path. The analysis scripts need only `numpy`.\n     9\t- **Precision.** Weights load in bf16 unless you ask otherwise; all distribution statistics are computed in float32 from the full logits row. Record any change (FP8 quantisation, dequantisation) in your submission.\n    10\t- **Paths.** The script defaults point at `/models/varentropy/...` on the original machine. Pass `--corpus` and `--out` explicitly. Two analysis scripts still have the path in their source: `vh_selfboot.py` (`corpus_path`) and `vh_tail.py` (`res`). Edit those two lines to point at your corpus file and results directory.\n    11\t\n    12\t## Corpora {#corpora}\n    13\t\n    14\tEvery model must read exactly the same documents, so download the published corpus files rather than rebuilding them. They live next to the data bundles, under `corpus/` in the bench's data bucket, with a `SHA256SUMS` file; check your copies against it before measuring. All four teacher-forced corpora are JSON files of the form `{\"tf\": [{\"idx\", \"set\", \"text\"}, ...], \"openers\": [...]}`.\n    15\t\n    16\t| file (flag) | pass | contents |\n    17\t|---|---|---|\n    18\t| `pile10k_sel.json` (`--corpus`) | `tf`, own-sample openers | 400 Pile documents plus 64 openers, a fixed seeded selection from NeelNanda/pile-10k |\n    19\t| `wiki_gh.json` (`--extra-corpus tf_wiki=PATH`) | `tf_wiki` | 150 Wikipedia and 60 GitHub documents |\n    20\t| `uncheatable_2026_04.json` (`--extra-corpus tf_fresh=PATH`) | `tf_fresh` | 900 post-cutoff documents in 15 categories (UncheatableEval 2026-04) |\n    21\t| `weird.json` (`--extra-corpus tf_weird=PATH`) | `tf_weird` | 199 documents from 12 rare Pile genres |\n    22\t| `names.json` (`--names-probe`) | surname probe | `{\"surnames\": [[name, count], ...]}` from the 2010 US Census, most common first |\n    23\t\n    24\t{{block:corpora}}\n    25\t\n    26\tIf `--corpus` points at a file that does not exist, `vh_score.py` builds the Pile selection from the pile-10k Arrow file given by `--arrow` (seed 0, documents of at least 400 characters) and writes it there. Only do this to reproduce the published file; a different selection makes your numbers incomparable.\n    27\t\n    28\tThe `tf_fresh` texts post-date the training cutoff of every model in v1. If your checkpoint's data extends past April 2026, its `tf_fresh` pass is no longer post-cutoff; say so in the pull request.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Run the passes: HF transformers {#run-hf}\n    31\t\n    32\tUse `vh_score.py` for dense checkpoints and MoE checkpoints that transformers loads natively.\n    33\t\n    34\t```bash\n    35\tpython docs/vh_score.py --model /path/to/checkpoint --name my-model \\\n    36\t  --out results --corpus corpus/pile10k_sel.json \\\n    37\t  --extra-corpus tf_wiki=corpus/wiki_gh.json \\\n    38\t  --extra-corpus tf_fresh=corpus/uncheatable_2026_04.json \\\n    39\t  --extra-corpus tf_weird=corpus/weird.json \\\n    40\t  --names-probe corpus/names.json --max-mem-gib 165\n    41\t```\n    42\t\n    43\tUseful flags:\n    44\t\n    45\t- `--max-mem-gib` caps the memory per GPU used for weights, leaving room for full-vocabulary logits.\n    46\t- `--trust-remote-code` for custom-code checkpoints; `--arch deepseek_v3` forces the native DeepSeek-V3 class for Kimi K2 and DeepSeek-V3-architecture checkpoints that ship custom code.\n    47\t- `--quantize-fp8` quantises a bf16 checkpoint to block-128 FP8 at load; `--fp8-dequantize` does the reverse for FP8 checkpoints the quantiser cannot wrap.\n    48\t- `--experts-impl` selects the transformers experts implementation for MoE models.\n    49\t- `--skip-tf` / `--skip-rollouts` rerun only part of a model. Extra corpora whose `.npz` already exists are skipped, so an interrupted run can be resumed.\n    50\t\n    51\t## Run the passes: vLLM {#run-vllm}\n    52\t\n    53\tUse `vh_score_vllm.py` for checkpoints transformers cannot load efficiently, such as FP8 custom-code MoE. It writes the same files as the HF path. Exact per-step statistics come from the logits processor in `vh_lp.py`; teacher forcing replays each document with the real token forced through the logits mask. vLLM loads the processor by module name in its worker processes, so put `docs/` on `PYTHONPATH`.\n    54\t\n    55\t```bash\n    56\tPYTHONPATH=docs python docs/vh_score_vllm.py --model /path/to/checkpoint --name my-model \\\n    57\t  --out results --corpus corpus/pile10k_sel.json --tp 8 --trust-remote-code \\\n    58\t  --extra-corpus tf_wiki=corpus/wiki_gh.json \\\n    59\t  --extra-corpus tf_fresh=corpus/uncheatable_2026_04.json \\\n    60\t  --extra-corpus tf_weird=corpus/weird.json \\\n    61\t  --names-probe corpus/names.json --rollout-steps 1024\n    62\t```\n    63\t\n    64\t- `--prepend-bos` adds the BOS token when the tokenizer does not (DeepSeek V4 needs it). Check that your tokenizer's first token on the corpus is BOS on either path.\n    65\t- `--rollout-steps 1024` is what the FP8 giants used in v1; the default and the reference value elsewhere is 1536. A shorter rollout lowers the collapse statistics' exposure and must be declared.\n    66\t- `--gpu-mem`, `--max-num-seqs`, `--max-model-len` and `--max-num-batched-tokens` are passed through to vLLM.\n    67\t- Prefix caching is disabled on purpose: each request must produce its own statistics.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Expected runtime {#runtime}\n    70\t\n    71\tThe work is fixed: about 290k Pile tokens, 1.3M post-cutoff tokens, the Wikipedia/GitHub and rare-genre sets, 64 sampled sequences of 1536 steps, 6 × 2,000 surname scorings and 6 × 16 sampled surname lists. Every position keeps the full vocabulary distribution, so throughput is lower than for an ordinary evaluation run. Runtime therefore scales with parameter count and vocabulary size: on the reference node, expect minutes to tens of minutes per pass for a small dense model and hours in total for the largest MoE checkpoints. On the vLLM path teacher forcing costs more than a single forward pass per document, because each document is replayed step by step. The load time is written to `run.json` in the result directory, and every phase logs its own timing.\n    72\t\n    73\t## Analyse {#analyse}\n    74\t\n     1\timport { useId, useState } from \"react\";\n     2\timport { useBundleState } from \"../data/BundleContext\";\n     3\timport { resolvePath } from \"../data/bundle\";\n     4\timport { fmtNum, fmtRaw, isNum, NULL_TITLE, unitOf, type Fmt } from \"../lib/numfmt\";\n     5\t\n     6\t/**\n     7\t * The only way prose shows a measurement (DESIGN §5). Renders a formatted value with a dotted underline;\n     8\t * hover/focus pops out its bundle path and raw value. `value` overrides resolution (for derived values,\n     9\t * describe the derivation in `path`). `def` marks a method constant (\"definition\").\n    10\t */\n    11\texport function Num({\n    12\t  path,\n    13\t  fmt = \"num3\",\n    14\t  value,\n    15\t  def = false,\n    16\t  unit = true,\n    17\t  derived,\n    18\t  nullText,\n    19\t  nullTitle,\n    20\t}: {\n    21\t  path: string;\n    22\t  fmt?: Fmt;\n    23\t  value?: number | null;\n    24\t  def?: boolean;\n    25\t  unit?: boolean;\n    26\t  derived?: string;\n    27\t  /** What a null means for this field, when it is not \"not measured\" (e.g. smith_plain). */\n    28\t  nullText?: string;\n    29\t  nullTitle?: string;\n    30\t}) {\n    31\t  const { state } = useBundleState();\n    32\t  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);\n    33\t  const id = useId();\n    34\t  let v: unknown = value;\n    35\t  if (value === undefined && state.status === \"ready\") v = resolvePath(state.loaded.bundle, path);\n    36\t  const num = isNum(v) ? v : null;\n    37\t  const text = num === null && nullText ? nullText : fmtNum(num, fmt);\n    38\t  const u = unit ? unitOf(fmt) : \"\";\n    39\t  return (\n    40\t    <span\n    41\t      className=\"num\"\n    42\t      tabIndex={0}\n    43\t      aria-describedby={open ? id : undefined}\n    44\t      title={num === null ? nullTitle ?? NULL_TITLE : undefined}\n    45\t      onMouseEnter={() => setOpen(true)}\n    46\t      onMouseLeave={() => setOpen(false)}\n    47\t      onFocus={() => setOpen(true)}\n    48\t      onBlur={() => setOpen(false)}\n    49\t      onKeyDown={(e) => e.key === \"Escape\" && setOpen(false)}\n    50\t    >\n    51\t      {text}\n    52\t      {u && num !== null && <span className=\"unit\">{u}</span>}\n    53\t      {open && (\n    54\t        <span className=\"pop\" role=\"tooltip\" id={id}>\n    55\t          {def && <span className=\"def\">definition · </span>}\n    56\t          {derived ? <>{derived} = </> : <>{path} = </>}\n    57\t          {fmtRaw(num ?? (v as unknown))}\n    58\t          {derived && (\n    59\t            <>\n    60\t              <br />\n    61\t              <span className=\"def\">from {path}</span>\n    62\t            </>\n    63\t          )}\n    64\t        </span>\n    65\t      )}\n    66\t    </span>\n    67\t  );\n    68\t}\n     1\timport {\n     2\t  createContext,\n     3\t  useCallback,\n     4\t  useContext,\n     5\t  useEffect,\n     6\t  useLayoutEffect,\n     7\t  useRef,\n     8\t  useState,\n     9\t  type KeyboardEvent,\n    10\t  type ReactNode,\n    11\t} from \"react\";\n    12\timport { useNavigate } from \"react-router-dom\";\n    13\t\n    14\texport function useWidth<T extends HTMLElement>(fallback = 640): [React.RefObject<T>, number] {\n    15\t  const ref = useRef<T>(null);\n    16\t  const [w, setW] = useState(fallback);\n    17\t  useLayoutEffect(() => {\n    18\t    const el = ref.current;\n    19\t    if (!el) return;\n    20\t    const set = () => setW(Math.max(240, Math.floor(el.getBoundingClientRect().width)));\n    21\t    set();\n    22\t    if (typeof ResizeObserver === \"undefined\") return;\n    23\t    const ro = new ResizeObserver(set);\n    24\t    ro.observe(el);\n    25\t    return () => ro.disconnect();\n    26\t  }, []);\n    27\t  return [ref, w];\n    28\t}\n    29\t\n    30\tinterface Tip {\n    31\t  x: number;\n    32\t  y: number;\n    33\t  content: ReactNode;\n    34\t}\n    35\tconst TipCtx = createContext<{ show: (t: Tip) => void; hide: () => void }>({ show: () => {}, hide: () => {} });\n    36\t\n    37\t/**\n    38\t * Responsive chart frame. Measures its width, renders children(width), hosts one tooltip, and gives\n    39\t * keyboard navigation between focusable marks (arrow keys) with Esc to close (DESIGN §5 Tooltip).\n    40\t */\n    41\texport function ChartFrame({\n    42\t  children,\n    43\t  maxWidth,\n    44\t  ariaLabel,\n    45\t  height,\n    46\t}: {\n    47\t  children: (width: number) => ReactNode;\n    48\t  maxWidth?: number;\n    49\t  ariaLabel: string;\n    50\t  height?: number;\n    51\t}) {\n    52\t  const [ref, width] = useWidth<HTMLDivElement>(maxWidth ?? 640);\n    53\t  const [tip, setTip] = useState<Tip | null>(null);\n    54\t  const show = useCallback((t: Tip) => setTip(t), []);\n    55\t  const hide = useCallback(() => setTip(null), []);\n    56\t  const w = maxWidth ? Math.min(width, maxWidth) : width;\n    57\t  // Roving tabindex: a chart is one Tab stop (the last-focused mark, else the first); arrow keys move between marks.\n    58\t  useEffect(() => {\n    59\t    const marks = Array.from(ref.current?.querySelectorAll<SVGElement>(\"[data-mark]\") ?? []);\n    60\t    if (!marks.length) return;\n    61\t    const active = marks.find((m) => m === document.activeElement) ?? marks.find((m) => m.dataset.roving === \"on\") ?? marks[0];\n    62\t    for (const m of marks) m.setAttribute(\"tabindex\", m === active ? \"0\" : \"-1\");\n    63\t  });\n    64\t  function onFocusIn(e: React.FocusEvent<HTMLDivElement>) {\n    65\t    const t = e.target as Element;\n    66\t    if (!t.hasAttribute?.(\"data-mark\")) return;\n    67\t    for (const m of Array.from(ref.current?.querySelectorAll<SVGElement>(\"[data-mark]\") ?? [])) {\n    68\t      const on = m === t;\n    69\t      m.setAttribute(\"tabindex\", on ? \"0\" : \"-1\");\n    70\t      if (on) m.dataset.roving = \"on\";\n    71\t      else delete m.dataset.roving;\n    72\t    }\n    73\t  }\n    74\t  function onKeyDown(e: KeyboardEvent<HTMLDivElement>) {\n    75\t    if (e.key === \"Escape\") {\n    76\t      hide();\n    77\t      return;\n    78\t    }\n    79\t    const keys = [\"ArrowRight\", \"ArrowDown\", \"ArrowLeft\", \"ArrowUp\"];\n    80\t    if (!keys.includes(e.key)) return;\n    81\t    const marks = Array.from(ref.current?.querySelectorAll<SVGElement>(\"[data-mark]\") ?? []);\n    82\t    const i = marks.indexOf(document.activeElement as SVGElement);\n    83\t    if (i < 0) return;\n    84\t    e.preventDefault();\n    85\t    const d = e.key === \"ArrowRight\" || e.key === \"ArrowDown\" ? 1 : -1;\n    86\t    marks[(i + d + marks.length) % marks.length]?.focus();\n    87\t  }\n    88\t  return (\n    89\t    <TipCtx.Provider value={{ show, hide }}>\n    90\t      <div className=\"chart-wrap\" ref={ref} onKeyDown={onKeyDown} onFocus={onFocusIn} onMouseLeave={hide} style={{ minHeight: height }}>\n    91\t        <div role=\"img\" aria-label={ariaLabel}>\n    92\t          {children(w)}\n    93\t        </div>\n    94\t        {tip && (\n    95\t          <div\n    96\t            className=\"tooltip\"\n    97\t            role=\"tooltip\"\n    98\t            style={{\n    99\t              left: Math.max(0, Math.min(tip.x + 10, w - 220)),\n   100\t              top: tip.y + 12,\n   101\t            }}\n   102\t          >\n   103\t            {tip.content}\n   104\t          </div>\n   105\t        )}\n   106\t      </div>\n   107\t    </TipCtx.Provider>\n   108\t  );\n   109\t}\n   110\t\n   111\t/** A focusable chart mark with tooltip; optional link target. */\n   112\texport function Mark({\n   113\t  x,\n   114\t  y,\n   115\t  tip,\n   116\t  href,\n   117\t  children,\n   118\t  label,\n   119\t  onHover,\n   120\t}: {\n   121\t  x: number;\n   122\t  y: number;\n   123\t  tip: ReactNode;\n   124\t  href?: string;\n   125\t  children: ReactNode;\n   126\t  label: string;\n   127\t  onHover?: (on: boolean) => void;\n   128\t}) {\n   129\t  const { show, hide } = useContext(TipCtx);\n   130\t  const navigate = useNavigate();\n   131\t  const on = () => {\n   132\t    show({ x, y, content: tip });\n   133\t    onHover?.(true);\n   134\t  };\n   135\t  const off = () => {\n   136\t    hide();\n   137\t    onHover?.(false);\n   138\t  };\n   139\t  return (\n   140\t    <g\n   141\t      data-mark=\"\"\n   142\t      tabIndex={0}\n   143\t      role={href ? \"link\" : \"img\"}\n   144\t      aria-label={label}\n   145\t      style={{ cursor: href ? \"pointer\" : \"default\", outline: \"none\" }}\n   146\t      className=\"mark\"\n   147\t      onMouseEnter={on}\n   148\t      onMouseLeave={off}\n   149\t      onFocus={on}\n   150\t      onBlur={off}\n   151\t      onClick={href ? () => navigate(href) : undefined}\n   152\t      onKeyDown={(e) => {\n   153\t        if (href && e.key === \"Enter\") navigate(href);\n   154\t      }}\n   155\t    >\n   156\t      {/* hit area */}\n   157\t      <circle cx={x} cy={y} r={9} fill=\"transparent\" />\n   158\t      {children}\n   159\t      <circle className=\"focus-ring\" cx={x} cy={y} r={8} fill=\"none\" />\n   160\t    </g>\n   161\t  );\n   162\t}\n   163\t\n   164\texport function TipBody({ title, lines, path }: { title: ReactNode; lines?: ReactNode[]; path?: string }) {\n   165\t  return (\n   166\t    <>\n   167\t      <div style={{ fontWeight: 600, color: \"var(--ink)\" }}>{title}</div>\n   168\t      {lines?.map((l, i) => (\n   169\t        <div key={i}>{l}</div>\n   170\t      ))}\n   171\t      {path && <div className=\"path\">{path}</div>}\n   172\t    </>\n   173\t  );\n   174\t}\n   175\t\n   176\texport function useMounted() {\n   177\t  const [m, setM] = useState(false);\n   178\t  useEffect(() => setM(true), []);\n   179\t  return m;\n   180\t}\n     1\timport { useEffect, useState } from \"react\";\n     2\timport { NavLink, useLocation } from \"react-router-dom\";\n     3\timport { useBundleState } from \"../data/BundleContext\";\n     4\timport { familiesOf } from \"../data/derive\";\n     5\timport { ORG_URL } from \"../lib/config\";\n     6\timport { useMotion } from \"../lib/motion\";\n     7\timport { applyTheme, loadTheme, saveTheme, type ThemePref } from \"../lib/prefs\";\n     8\t\n     9\texport function RampGlyph({ size = 16 }: { size?: number }) {\n    10\t  return (\n    11\t    <svg width={size} height={size} viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n    12\t      <path d=\"M1 4.5 H5.5 L10.5 11.5 H15\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--ink)\" strokeWidth=\"1.5\" strokeLinejoin=\"round\" strokeLinecap=\"round\" />\n    13\t    </svg>\n    14\t  );\n    15\t}\n    16\t\n    17\tlet themeState: ThemePref | null = null;\n    18\tconst listeners = new Set<(t: ThemePref) => void>();\n    19\tfunction useTheme(): [ThemePref, (t: ThemePref) => void] {\n    20\t  const [t, setT] = useState<ThemePref>(() => (themeState ??= loadTheme()));\n    21\t  useEffect(() => {\n    22\t    listeners.add(setT);\n    23\t    return () => {\n    24\t      listeners.delete(setT);\n    25\t    };\n    26\t  }, []);\n    27\t  return [\n    28\t    t,\n    29\t    (n) => {\n    30\t      themeState = n;\n    31\t      saveTheme(n);\n    32\t      listeners.forEach((l) => l(n));\n    33\t    },\n    34\t  ];\n    35\t}\n    36\t\n    37\texport function ThemeSwitch() {\n    38\t  const [theme, setTheme] = useTheme();\n    39\t  useEffect(() => applyTheme(theme), [theme]);\n    40\t  return (\n    41\t    <div className=\"seg theme-switch\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Theme\">\n    42\t      {([\"system\", \"light\", \"dark\"] as ThemePref[]).map((t) => (\n    43\t        <button key={t} type=\"button\" className=\"btn\" aria-pressed={theme === t} onClick={() => setTheme(t)}>\n    44\t          {t[0].toUpperCase() + t.slice(1)}\n    45\t        </button>\n    46\t      ))}\n    47\t    </div>\n    48\t  );\n    49\t}\n    50\t\n    51\tconst NAV = [\n    52\t  { to: \"/\", label: \"Leaderboard\", end: true },\n    53\t  { to: \"/findings\", label: \"Findings\" },\n    54\t  { to: \"/methodology\", label: \"Methodology\" },\n    55\t  { to: \"/measure\", label: \"Measure your model\" },\n    56\t];\n    57\t\n    58\tfunction Stamp() {\n    59\t  const { state } = useBundleState();\n    60\t  if (state.status !== \"ready\") return <span className=\"stamp\" />;\n    61\t  const { bundle, file } = state.loaded;\n    62\t  return (\n    63\t    <span className=\"stamp\" title={`${file} · schema_version ${bundle.schema_version}`}>\n    64\t      {file.replace(/\\.json$/, \"\")} · {bundle.generated} · {bundle.n_models} models\n    65\t    </span>\n    66\t  );\n    67\t}\n    68\t\n    69\texport function TopBar() {\n    70\t  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);\n    71\t  const loc = useLocation();\n    72\t  useEffect(() => setOpen(false), [loc.pathname]);\n    73\t  return (\n    74\t    <header className=\"topbar\">\n    75\t      <div className=\"container\">\n    76\t        <NavLink to=\"/\" className=\"wordmark\" aria-label=\"True Base Bench, home\">\n    77\t          <RampGlyph />\n    78\t          True Base Bench\n    79\t        </NavLink>\n    80\t        <a className=\"byline\" href={ORG_URL}>\n    81\t          by Anima Labs\n    82\t        </a>\n    83\t        <button type=\"button\" className=\"btn menu-btn\" aria-expanded={open} aria-controls=\"site-nav\" onClick={() => setOpen(!open)}>\n    84\t          Menu\n    85\t        </button>\n    86\t        <nav id=\"site-nav\" className={\"nav\" + (open ? \" open\" : \"\")} aria-label=\"Main\">\n    87\t          {NAV.map((n) => (\n    88\t            <NavLink key={n.to} to={n.to} end={n.end}>\n    89\t              {n.label}\n    90\t            </NavLink>\n    91\t          ))}\n    92\t        </nav>\n    93\t        <span className=\"spacer\" />\n    94\t        <Stamp />\n    95\t        <ThemeSwitch />\n    96\t      </div>\n    97\t    </header>\n    98\t  );\n    99\t}\n   100\t\n   101\texport function Footer() {\n   102\t  const { state } = useBundleState();\n   103\t  const { toggle, setToggle } = useMotion();\n   104\t  const ready = state.status === \"ready\" ? state.loaded : null;\n   105\t  return (\n   106\t    <footer className=\"footer\">\n   107\t      <div className=\"container\">\n   108\t        <div className=\"row\">\n   109\t          <span>\n   110\t            True Base Bench by <a href={ORG_URL}>Anima Labs</a>\n   111\t          </span>\n   112\t          <NavLink to=\"/about\">About, citation and maintenance</NavLink>\n   113\t        </div>\n   114\t        {ready && (\n   115\t          <div className=\"row mono\">\n   116\t            <span>{ready.file}</span>\n   117\t            <span>schema_version {ready.bundle.schema_version}</span>\n   118\t            <span>generated {ready.bundle.generated}</span>\n   119\t            <span>n_models {ready.bundle.n_models}</span>\n   120\t            <span>n_families {familiesOf(ready.bundle).length}</span>\n   121\t            <a href={ready.url} download>\n   122\t              Download bundle (JSON)\n   123\t            </a>\n   124\t          </div>\n   125\t        )}\n   126\t        <div className=\"row\">\n   127\t          <span className=\"ink3\">Data: OVH Object Storage, manifest.json</span>\n   128\t          {ready?.fallback && <span className=\"ink3\">(showing the bundled copy)</span>}\n   129\t        </div>\n   130\t        <div className=\"row\">\n   131\t          <ThemeSwitch />\n   132\t          <button type=\"button\" className=\"btn\" aria-pressed={toggle} onClick={() => setToggle(!toggle)}>\n   133\t            Reduce motion\n   134\t          </button>\n   135\t        </div>\n   136\t      </div>\n   137\t    </footer>\n   138\t  );\n   139\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
